WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.175
&gt;&gt; So, welcome to

00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:11.804
the session on verification
and secure systems.

00:00:11.805 --> 00:00:14.655
So, we're going to have
three speakers on this.

00:00:14.656 --> 00:00:16.880
So, Ed Nightingale is going to

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:19.845
talk about Azure Sphere
and Secure Hardware.

00:00:19.846 --> 00:00:22.940
Then Adams Apollo from MIT,

00:00:22.941 --> 00:00:24.050
is going to be talking about

00:00:24.051 --> 00:00:26.795
Formal Methods and how
that drives system design.

00:00:26.796 --> 00:00:28.730
Then finally, Nick Swamy

00:00:28.731 --> 00:00:31.050
from MS Labs is
going to be talking

00:00:31.051 --> 00:00:35.990
about the Everest Project
and Verifying TLS.

00:00:35.991 --> 00:00:38.370
So, let me go ahead and
turn it over to Ed.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:39.760
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Chris.
You guys hear me?

00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:41.560
Good morning, it's
my pleasure to be here.

00:00:41.561 --> 00:00:43.570
My name is Ed Nightingale,

00:00:43.571 --> 00:00:45.510
I'm one of the members of
the Azure Sphere Team.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:48.820
This is a product that was
just announced this Spring.

00:00:48.821 --> 00:00:50.090
This talk might be a bit of

00:00:50.091 --> 00:00:51.440
a different altitude that I might

00:00:51.441 --> 00:00:52.975
normally give at
a faculty summit,

00:00:52.976 --> 00:00:54.230
we're really looking for

00:00:54.231 --> 00:00:55.940
some engagement with
the academic communities

00:00:55.941 --> 00:00:57.200
and feedback and I
love to talk to you

00:00:57.201 --> 00:00:59.175
all later in the day.

00:00:59.176 --> 00:01:02.020
So, and actually right now,

00:01:02.021 --> 00:01:03.140
you can order development kit

00:01:03.141 --> 00:01:04.040
if you're interested
in playing with

00:01:04.041 --> 00:01:05.150
the Azure Sphere Product

00:01:05.151 --> 00:01:07.675
or trying to hack
it, we'd love that.

00:01:07.676 --> 00:01:09.620
It's what's Azure
Sphere focused on.

00:01:09.621 --> 00:01:13.040
Azure Sphere is focused all
around microcontrollers.

00:01:13.041 --> 00:01:17.270
Microcontrollers are
low-cost single-chip computers.

00:01:17.271 --> 00:01:19.415
Microcontrollers are really in

00:01:19.416 --> 00:01:20.995
all the devices around you,

00:01:20.996 --> 00:01:23.215
in your home, in your hotel room,

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:26.025
at work that you
never think about.

00:01:26.026 --> 00:01:30.035
There are nine billion
MCU based devices

00:01:30.036 --> 00:01:31.125
built and deployed every year,

00:01:31.126 --> 00:01:33.335
more than one per
person on the planet.

00:01:33.336 --> 00:01:35.555
If it has a screen,
if it has a button,

00:01:35.556 --> 00:01:37.915
or a low-risk screen, it's
got a microcontroller on it.

00:01:37.916 --> 00:01:40.885
Now, what's the big change?

00:01:40.886 --> 00:01:42.585
About four years ago,

00:01:42.586 --> 00:01:47.040
you started to see
microcontrollers with a radio,

00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:54.055
and a chip like this
is around $2.50.

00:01:54.056 --> 00:01:57.280
So, if you said, "Well,
for $2.50 I can take

00:01:57.281 --> 00:01:58.910
those nine billion devices

00:01:58.911 --> 00:02:00.530
and connect them all
to the Internet."

00:02:00.531 --> 00:02:02.995
I think for most of
this device you say,

00:02:02.996 --> 00:02:04.560
obviously, we should
connect them,

00:02:04.561 --> 00:02:06.800
it enables us to produce
new experiences,

00:02:06.801 --> 00:02:08.720
enables us to interact
with them live in

00:02:08.721 --> 00:02:09.770
a new connections with

00:02:09.771 --> 00:02:12.065
the people who are
using those devices.

00:02:12.066 --> 00:02:14.330
Now, the interesting
issue is that

00:02:14.331 --> 00:02:15.960
only about one percent

00:02:15.961 --> 00:02:18.380
of these MCU devices are
connected right now.

00:02:18.381 --> 00:02:20.045
So, why is that?

00:02:20.046 --> 00:02:22.820
Well, you have this huge
opportunity connectivity

00:02:22.821 --> 00:02:24.530
and you also have
a large amount of risk.

00:02:24.531 --> 00:02:26.150
We'll just gives you
a simple example.

00:02:26.151 --> 00:02:27.710
I think everyone in this room has

00:02:27.711 --> 00:02:29.405
used refrigerator and has one,

00:02:29.406 --> 00:02:31.850
and a simple example
for connectivity is,

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:33.215
how do you know
whether the compressor

00:02:33.216 --> 00:02:35.045
has failed in your refrigerator?

00:02:35.046 --> 00:02:37.225
Well, without connectivity,
you wake up in the morning,

00:02:37.226 --> 00:02:39.275
you've got melted ice cream
all over the floor.

00:02:39.276 --> 00:02:41.570
With connectivity and
predictive maintenance,

00:02:41.571 --> 00:02:43.220
you might get a phone call from

00:02:43.221 --> 00:02:45.515
your device manufacturer and
they say, "You know what?

00:02:45.516 --> 00:02:47.600
We've noticed your compressor
starting to fail;

00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:49.485
can we stop by and replace it?

00:02:49.486 --> 00:02:52.685
"Obviously, that's
a better experience

00:02:52.686 --> 00:02:55.025
than the one with
the melted ice cream.

00:02:55.026 --> 00:02:58.040
But what happens when

00:02:58.041 --> 00:02:59.510
you connect the device
to the Internet?

00:02:59.511 --> 00:03:00.450
Well, we all know
what happens if you

00:03:00.451 --> 00:03:01.685
connect a device the Internet,

00:03:01.686 --> 00:03:03.675
we've seen all the stories,

00:03:03.676 --> 00:03:07.235
you've seen baby monitors
turned into spy devices,

00:03:07.236 --> 00:03:09.450
you've seen refrigerators
are sending spam,

00:03:09.451 --> 00:03:11.735
and you've seen
ransomware attacks,

00:03:11.736 --> 00:03:15.505
hackers infect millions of
devices all over the world.

00:03:15.506 --> 00:03:17.780
The other day I actually
took my router and

00:03:17.781 --> 00:03:19.460
threw it at the trash
because the FBI,

00:03:19.461 --> 00:03:21.250
so that's what I should do
with it because I couldn't

00:03:21.251 --> 00:03:24.325
update the software remotely,
never done that before.

00:03:24.326 --> 00:03:28.310
We're in a space where we have
this huge opportunity for

00:03:28.311 --> 00:03:29.930
low-cost connectivity and we

00:03:29.931 --> 00:03:31.740
have this massive amount of risk.

00:03:31.741 --> 00:03:33.420
A really good example,

00:03:33.421 --> 00:03:36.120
this is the Mirai
Botnet from 2016.

00:03:36.121 --> 00:03:37.550
The Mirai botnet, these are

00:03:37.551 --> 00:03:40.220
just simple IP cameras and

00:03:40.221 --> 00:03:41.610
took down basically the Internet

00:03:41.611 --> 00:03:43.150
on the East Coast of
the United States.

00:03:43.151 --> 00:03:45.900
Now, what's scary about
the Mirai botnet?

00:03:45.901 --> 00:03:47.884
It's only a 100,000 devices,

00:03:47.885 --> 00:03:49.750
it's a tiny number of devices.

00:03:49.751 --> 00:03:51.290
If you look at one market for

00:03:51.291 --> 00:03:53.970
connectivity like
major appliances,

00:03:53.971 --> 00:03:57.050
there are about 350
million major appliances

00:03:57.051 --> 00:03:58.250
sold every year around the world.

00:03:58.251 --> 00:04:00.200
A hundred thousand devices
can take down

00:04:00.201 --> 00:04:02.480
a portion of Internet's
infrastructure,

00:04:02.481 --> 00:04:06.095
imagine a billion refrigerators
involved beside the DDoS.

00:04:06.096 --> 00:04:07.970
The other sad thing about

00:04:07.971 --> 00:04:09.950
this attack is
well-known weakness.

00:04:09.951 --> 00:04:11.950
I believe it was
a fixed password.

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.580
The password was shockingly

00:04:13.581 --> 00:04:16.500
R-O-O-T. You couldn't change

00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:18.360
the password, and even worse,

00:04:18.361 --> 00:04:20.240
once they saw
the exploit happening,

00:04:20.241 --> 00:04:21.530
there was no way to update

00:04:21.531 --> 00:04:23.170
the software on it
to get rid of it,

00:04:23.171 --> 00:04:25.810
it was just a mini-disaster.

00:04:25.811 --> 00:04:28.900
Another great example
recently is that there is

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:32.485
a connected thermometer
in a fish tank,

00:04:32.486 --> 00:04:34.320
similarly they able to hack it,

00:04:34.321 --> 00:04:36.940
and they were able to
actually use this to bounce

00:04:36.941 --> 00:04:38.410
over and attack at

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.685
database and get access
to high roller data.

00:04:40.686 --> 00:04:43.345
So, we're in the Wild West.

00:04:43.346 --> 00:04:45.520
We're in a space with IoT where

00:04:45.521 --> 00:04:48.825
security is an afterthought,
what are we going to do?

00:04:48.826 --> 00:04:51.370
Well, the first thing
we're going to do

00:04:51.371 --> 00:04:52.390
is when we talk about

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:54.220
connectivity to major
manufacturers is,

00:04:54.221 --> 00:04:57.355
why I'm going to beat
this dead horse?

00:04:57.356 --> 00:04:59.150
Imagine that refrigerator,

00:04:59.151 --> 00:05:00.130
you wake up in
the morning but instead

00:05:00.131 --> 00:05:02.390
of melted ice cream you got
a text message on your phone,

00:05:02.391 --> 00:05:04.205
it says, "We found your fridge,

00:05:04.206 --> 00:05:07.750
send us Bitcoin or all
your ice cream melt."

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.860
The more important
one I think is,

00:05:11.861 --> 00:05:13.310
you wake up in the morning

00:05:13.311 --> 00:05:15.225
and 500,000 stoves are blown up.

00:05:15.226 --> 00:05:18.770
There's actually a customer
we spoke with who had a stove

00:05:18.771 --> 00:05:20.450
that was in development
and they did

00:05:20.451 --> 00:05:22.340
a security audit after
we talked to them,

00:05:22.341 --> 00:05:24.230
they figured out that
through a software

00:05:24.231 --> 00:05:26.840
exploits they could turn
on the gas in the stove,

00:05:26.841 --> 00:05:28.550
and an arbitrary
amount of time later,

00:05:28.551 --> 00:05:29.935
they could light it.

00:05:29.936 --> 00:05:32.775
So, that did not hit
the market, thankfully.

00:05:32.776 --> 00:05:35.090
They caught that but
the risks are really real,

00:05:35.091 --> 00:05:37.900
the risks are about physical
harm. Is this foundational?

00:05:37.901 --> 00:05:39.215
I think everyone in this room

00:05:39.216 --> 00:05:41.215
knows that security
is foundational,

00:05:41.216 --> 00:05:43.775
and I think the issue is
really trying to create

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:47.520
platforms where security
comes from first principles.

00:05:47.521 --> 00:05:49.160
So, what's Microsoft
doing about this?

00:05:49.161 --> 00:05:50.840
What it's security
about? Well, there's

00:05:50.841 --> 00:05:51.890
two things I want to talk about.

00:05:51.891 --> 00:05:54.850
One, is that [inaudible]

00:05:54.851 --> 00:05:56.170
authored a white paper called

00:05:56.171 --> 00:05:58.475
The Seven Properties of
Highly Secure Devices.

00:05:58.476 --> 00:06:01.160
These properties are measurable.
Now, why did we do this?

00:06:01.161 --> 00:06:03.035
Well, we felt that there is

00:06:03.036 --> 00:06:05.900
no bar someone can
look at when they

00:06:05.901 --> 00:06:07.250
really are looking at

00:06:07.251 --> 00:06:08.690
an IoT solution and

00:06:08.691 --> 00:06:10.895
determine the level
of security they had.

00:06:10.896 --> 00:06:13.505
Everyone's simply
says, "You're secure."

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:15.090
It's black and white,
it's a single bit.

00:06:15.091 --> 00:06:16.160
Everyone in this room knows

00:06:16.161 --> 00:06:17.580
that's not how security works.

00:06:17.581 --> 00:06:20.350
This is us putting
a line of their marker,

00:06:20.351 --> 00:06:22.325
this is the minimum bar,

00:06:22.326 --> 00:06:24.440
any device that's connected
to the Internet should

00:06:24.441 --> 00:06:27.040
meet to have the potential
to be secured.

00:06:27.041 --> 00:06:28.110
So, you have a lot of
basics here, right?

00:06:28.111 --> 00:06:29.460
You have a hardware
root of trust.

00:06:29.461 --> 00:06:31.585
We have things like
defense in depth,

00:06:31.586 --> 00:06:33.925
keeping your trusted
computing-based small,

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:35.550
certificate-based authentication,

00:06:35.551 --> 00:06:37.770
failed reporting, and
renewable security.

00:06:37.771 --> 00:06:42.370
So, this is up on the web
at aka.ms/7properties.

00:06:42.371 --> 00:06:43.720
We'd love for you guys to read,

00:06:43.721 --> 00:06:45.020
we'd love feedback
about it and have

00:06:45.021 --> 00:06:47.370
a conversation about
these seven properties,

00:06:47.371 --> 00:06:49.160
what those properties provide,

00:06:49.161 --> 00:06:50.450
and why do you think
we've hit the mark there,

00:06:50.451 --> 00:06:51.785
or you think that there's room

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:53.785
for more discussion
engagement here.

00:06:53.786 --> 00:06:55.100
One of most important things
though is that

00:06:55.101 --> 00:06:56.420
someone who's not
a security expert

00:06:56.421 --> 00:06:57.770
at the very least could

00:06:57.771 --> 00:06:59.480
take the Seven Properties
and just ask,

00:06:59.481 --> 00:07:01.195
do I have the Seven Properties?

00:07:01.196 --> 00:07:03.330
Do I have a hardware root
of trust in my device?

00:07:03.331 --> 00:07:04.820
Do I have certificate-based
authentication?

00:07:04.821 --> 00:07:07.525
If they don't, they can
start to ask, why not?

00:07:07.526 --> 00:07:09.775
So, that's properties,

00:07:09.776 --> 00:07:11.900
I want to talk in detail
about a couple of them.

00:07:11.901 --> 00:07:13.340
Some of these properties only

00:07:13.341 --> 00:07:14.840
depend on hardware
support and this is where

00:07:14.841 --> 00:07:16.130
we're going to get
into the need for

00:07:16.131 --> 00:07:18.935
secured silicon in addition
to software and services.

00:07:18.936 --> 00:07:21.180
Class examples are
hardware root of trust.

00:07:21.181 --> 00:07:23.395
It's unforgeable
cryptographic key,

00:07:23.396 --> 00:07:26.440
protects the hardware's identity,
so it can't be spoofed.

00:07:26.441 --> 00:07:28.990
Implements hardware for
things like secure boot,

00:07:28.991 --> 00:07:30.620
so we guarantee they're
only sign software

00:07:30.621 --> 00:07:32.000
runs on a device
and you eliminate

00:07:32.001 --> 00:07:34.040
the possibility of random malware

00:07:34.041 --> 00:07:35.705
being loaded and executed.

00:07:35.706 --> 00:07:38.430
Some properties depend upon
hardware and software.

00:07:38.431 --> 00:07:40.725
So for example, here's
a Dynamic Compartments.

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:43.040
Dynamic Compartment
you could think of as

00:07:43.041 --> 00:07:45.450
your MMU that provides

00:07:45.451 --> 00:07:48.710
address space translation and

00:07:48.711 --> 00:07:50.755
process-based isolation
in operating system.

00:07:50.756 --> 00:07:51.890
So, they made
compartments, I mean

00:07:51.891 --> 00:07:53.659
these compartments can
change on the fly,

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:55.595
you can update them
and change them later

00:07:55.596 --> 00:07:59.170
and provides protection
from one process,

00:07:59.171 --> 00:08:02.350
as example, nothing I'll
take down another one.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:06.105
So, last, some properties
depend upon hardware,

00:08:06.106 --> 00:08:07.960
software, and the Cloud.

00:08:07.961 --> 00:08:09.700
So, as an example here,

00:08:09.701 --> 00:08:12.005
we think about renewable
security, what does that mean?

00:08:12.006 --> 00:08:15.140
Simply means that we know it's
actually going to happen,

00:08:15.141 --> 00:08:16.655
we know exploits can be found,

00:08:16.656 --> 00:08:18.680
does an IoT device
have the ability

00:08:18.681 --> 00:08:21.065
to update its software
remotely and securely?

00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:23.790
This requires Cloud to
provide the updates,

00:08:23.791 --> 00:08:25.905
software to apply that update,

00:08:25.906 --> 00:08:27.915
and hardware to provide
rollback attacks.

00:08:27.916 --> 00:08:29.120
What's a rollback attack?

00:08:29.121 --> 00:08:32.930
Rollback attack is a really
clever attack where you have

00:08:32.931 --> 00:08:35.240
an old version of
let's say sign of

00:08:35.241 --> 00:08:37.640
legitimate software that
has a known vulnerability,

00:08:37.641 --> 00:08:39.860
and you trick the device
into basically

00:08:39.861 --> 00:08:41.810
downgrading to a legitimate
but buggy version

00:08:41.811 --> 00:08:43.280
of software so you
can exploit it.

00:08:43.281 --> 00:08:46.115
You have to have support in
your hardware root of trust,

00:08:46.116 --> 00:08:47.820
for example, and refuses to

00:08:47.821 --> 00:08:50.690
build prevent rollback
attacks from occurring.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:54.700
Those are the properties.
What's the product?

00:08:54.701 --> 00:08:55.955
What did we announce?

00:08:55.956 --> 00:08:57.945
Azure Sphere is
an end-to-end solution

00:08:57.946 --> 00:09:00.025
that's composed of three pillars.

00:09:00.026 --> 00:09:02.560
We have Azure
Sphere Certified MCUs.

00:09:02.561 --> 00:09:03.770
I'm going to give
a deep dive on what

00:09:03.771 --> 00:09:05.110
a Certified MCU means,

00:09:05.111 --> 00:09:07.445
what properties does MCUs have.

00:09:07.446 --> 00:09:12.045
An operating system, and
I said security services.

00:09:12.046 --> 00:09:15.060
So, the Certified MCU
is provided by

00:09:15.061 --> 00:09:16.380
silicon partners with built-in

00:09:16.381 --> 00:09:17.915
Microsoft security technology,

00:09:17.916 --> 00:09:19.720
provides connectivity
and guarantees

00:09:19.721 --> 00:09:22.100
a dependable hardware to trust.

00:09:22.101 --> 00:09:25.465
The Operating System is
secured by Microsoft,

00:09:25.466 --> 00:09:26.880
guarantee we're going
to provide updates

00:09:26.881 --> 00:09:28.250
for at least 10 years,

00:09:28.251 --> 00:09:30.200
and it provides
a trustworthy platform

00:09:30.201 --> 00:09:32.320
for IoT experiences.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:34.560
The Security Service.

00:09:34.561 --> 00:09:36.800
The Security Service
brokers trust between devices,

00:09:36.801 --> 00:09:38.315
between devices in the cloud,

00:09:38.316 --> 00:09:39.850
looks for emerging
threats through

00:09:39.851 --> 00:09:42.020
failure reporting and allows
for renewable security.

00:09:42.021 --> 00:09:45.340
I'll talk about that brokering
trust in a little bit.

00:09:45.341 --> 00:09:51.435
So, let's get into what an
Azure Sphere certified MCU is.

00:09:51.436 --> 00:09:54.965
Here's a term crossover
MCU in this slide.

00:09:54.966 --> 00:09:57.365
All these Azure
Sphere MCUs are connected.

00:09:57.366 --> 00:09:58.570
They have built-in networking.

00:09:58.571 --> 00:09:59.920
They're secured with

00:09:59.921 --> 00:10:02.895
this built-in security
technology which we call Pluton,

00:10:02.896 --> 00:10:04.820
and it's a crossover.
What does that mean?

00:10:04.821 --> 00:10:07.235
When you look at a typical MCU,
if you're in the space,

00:10:07.236 --> 00:10:09.050
you're talking about
something like a Cortex-M.

00:10:09.051 --> 00:10:11.510
Cortex-M has physical
address in it.

00:10:11.511 --> 00:10:14.630
It lacks a lot of the properties
we're looking for.

00:10:14.631 --> 00:10:15.820
The crossover MCU have

00:10:15.821 --> 00:10:17.455
both the Cortex-M
and the Cortex-A.

00:10:17.456 --> 00:10:20.230
Cortex-A gives us address
speed, address translation,

00:10:20.231 --> 00:10:22.790
let's us run a high-low
operating system

00:10:22.791 --> 00:10:24.880
based on a Linux kernel,

00:10:24.881 --> 00:10:29.110
has SRAM Flash, and is protected
by a bunch of what we're

00:10:29.111 --> 00:10:31.250
calling silicon
countermeasures that help

00:10:31.251 --> 00:10:34.670
guard against software that
ends up being exploited.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:38.895
So Pluton is the hardware root
of trust. What does it do?

00:10:38.896 --> 00:10:40.985
It guarantees authenticity
of your software.

00:10:40.986 --> 00:10:43.110
So it implements
Secure Boot through ECDSA.

00:10:43.111 --> 00:10:46.750
It protects against
downgrade attacks.

00:10:46.751 --> 00:10:49.365
It has the ability to view fuses,

00:10:49.366 --> 00:10:51.790
protect against
an old but buggy version

00:10:51.791 --> 00:10:54.115
of software ever being
run on the device,

00:10:54.116 --> 00:10:57.000
protects the authenticity
of the device itself.

00:10:57.001 --> 00:11:01.465
So it has its own
cryptographically-based identity,

00:11:01.466 --> 00:11:02.730
which means you can always
tell when the device

00:11:02.731 --> 00:11:05.180
connects whether it
is who it says it is.

00:11:05.181 --> 00:11:07.215
Reduces supply chain risk.

00:11:07.216 --> 00:11:09.390
What do I mean by this?
It's a great story

00:11:09.391 --> 00:11:11.570
you guys can look up out
there of a certain model of

00:11:11.571 --> 00:11:16.775
phone that one of the warehouses
was physically attacked,

00:11:16.776 --> 00:11:21.090
and they rooted 52 models of
phone and put malware on it.

00:11:21.091 --> 00:11:23.415
So when these phones
arrived at businesses,

00:11:23.416 --> 00:11:25.385
they came out of the box

00:11:25.386 --> 00:11:27.865
with the cameras on and
the microphones recording.

00:11:27.866 --> 00:11:30.450
So having Secure Boot

00:11:30.451 --> 00:11:33.365
guaranteeing that every piece
of software is signed

00:11:33.366 --> 00:11:35.000
eliminating the ability
to have something

00:11:35.001 --> 00:11:36.840
like a rooted or developed

00:11:36.841 --> 00:11:38.360
mode that someone
can arbitrarily turn

00:11:38.361 --> 00:11:41.015
on eliminates that type of risk.

00:11:41.016 --> 00:11:43.940
The Pluton and Azure Sphere

00:11:43.941 --> 00:11:46.090
MCUs are known about

00:11:46.091 --> 00:11:48.435
from the time of their birth
at the silicon manufacturer.

00:11:48.436 --> 00:11:50.465
So as that chip comes
out of the foundry,

00:11:50.466 --> 00:11:51.790
we know about its identity.

00:11:51.791 --> 00:11:53.410
We register that chip,
and then we can

00:11:53.411 --> 00:11:55.565
basically know about the chip
throughout its lifetime,

00:11:55.566 --> 00:11:56.980
which means we can tell what

00:11:56.981 --> 00:11:59.225
an authentic chip
is and what isn't.

00:11:59.226 --> 00:12:02.290
Now, it accelerates
common cryptographic tasks

00:12:02.291 --> 00:12:04.730
like ECC and EFS.

00:12:04.731 --> 00:12:06.450
Protects against
low-entropy attacks.

00:12:06.451 --> 00:12:09.135
So Pluton is a true
random number generator.

00:12:09.136 --> 00:12:10.825
It measures zone entropy.

00:12:10.826 --> 00:12:12.120
So it looks, for example,

00:12:12.121 --> 00:12:13.810
you cool the chip down to try to

00:12:13.811 --> 00:12:16.450
reduce the space which is
generating random numbers,

00:12:16.451 --> 00:12:18.530
it actually shuts itself off.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.350
So that's all about Pluton.

00:12:22.351 --> 00:12:25.015
There's the guarantees
that it provides.

00:12:25.016 --> 00:12:28.290
A little bit more detail
for this audience.

00:12:28.291 --> 00:12:32.045
It has a dedicated core
and it's owned

00:12:32.046 --> 00:12:33.820
TCM memory that resists

00:12:33.821 --> 00:12:36.405
side-channel attacks that
focus on a single core.

00:12:36.406 --> 00:12:38.905
It's a true random
number generator,

00:12:38.906 --> 00:12:42.010
and also implements remote boot,

00:12:42.011 --> 00:12:45.330
measured booting and
remote attestation in silicon.

00:12:45.331 --> 00:12:47.270
So it uses a digest
accumulator register

00:12:47.271 --> 00:12:48.930
and a nonce register
which allow us to

00:12:48.931 --> 00:12:50.750
implement remote attestation with

00:12:50.751 --> 00:12:52.330
their services and look at

00:12:52.331 --> 00:12:55.190
what software is actually
running on the device.

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:58.650
So why do we have Cortex-A?

00:12:58.651 --> 00:13:00.680
I get this question
a lot from folks

00:13:00.681 --> 00:13:03.230
who are in the MCU space.

00:13:03.231 --> 00:13:06.730
Cortex-A is actually
bigger in area.

00:13:06.731 --> 00:13:08.670
You should end up using
a little bit more memory

00:13:08.671 --> 00:13:09.945
if you are running
an OS on there.

00:13:09.946 --> 00:13:13.090
We think about security,
portability, extensibility here.

00:13:13.091 --> 00:13:15.890
So security, basically,
because we get isolation.

00:13:15.891 --> 00:13:18.290
Cortex-M chips don't have MMUs.

00:13:18.291 --> 00:13:19.835
They don't have
address-space translation.

00:13:19.836 --> 00:13:20.990
Having Cortex-A allows you

00:13:20.991 --> 00:13:23.030
to have process-based isolation,

00:13:23.031 --> 00:13:24.840
and I mean

00:13:24.841 --> 00:13:28.505
the seven properties for
dynamic compartmentalization.

00:13:28.506 --> 00:13:30.530
Also, there's a special
operating system.

00:13:30.531 --> 00:13:31.670
I'll get into details
a little bit.

00:13:31.671 --> 00:13:34.660
So the OS includes
our own runtime and

00:13:34.661 --> 00:13:36.060
a custom Linux kernel that's

00:13:36.061 --> 00:13:39.670
been focused on the IoT space.

00:13:39.671 --> 00:13:41.010
So as an example,

00:13:41.011 --> 00:13:42.950
we talked about
those default passwords.

00:13:42.951 --> 00:13:45.730
The Azure Sphere operating
system doesn't have passwords,

00:13:45.731 --> 00:13:47.060
and it doesn't have a shell.

00:13:47.061 --> 00:13:48.520
It doesn't have user accounts.

00:13:48.521 --> 00:13:50.370
Basically, took a portion
of this space that you

00:13:50.371 --> 00:13:52.250
typically have in the OS
and just excised it,

00:13:52.251 --> 00:13:54.390
which means we took
that attack surface out of

00:13:54.391 --> 00:13:57.970
the equation. Of
course, authentication.

00:13:57.971 --> 00:13:59.720
So we're doing mutual

00:13:59.721 --> 00:14:02.570
author certificate-based
authentication.

00:14:02.571 --> 00:14:05.670
We have our own
authorization mechanism

00:14:05.671 --> 00:14:07.710
that's rooted in Pluton
to give permissions to

00:14:07.711 --> 00:14:14.250
access to hardware
resources like peripherals.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:19.890
So this is basic for those
who focus in the OS space,

00:14:19.891 --> 00:14:21.865
desktop or server
operating systems,

00:14:21.866 --> 00:14:24.190
but having an address-based
virtualization

00:14:24.191 --> 00:14:26.420
means application code
just gets written once,

00:14:26.421 --> 00:14:28.100
compiled through
platforms and runs.

00:14:28.101 --> 00:14:30.025
This is not something you
typically have in an MCU.

00:14:30.026 --> 00:14:32.320
With a physical addressing,
you're typically rewriting,

00:14:32.321 --> 00:14:33.330
importing your application to

00:14:33.331 --> 00:14:35.215
every single MCU platform
that you port.

00:14:35.216 --> 00:14:38.560
So it's a much slower process
for innovation.

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:41.775
Source portability.
We have Linux kernel.

00:14:41.776 --> 00:14:43.400
We're focusing on OSS.

00:14:43.401 --> 00:14:45.400
Most libraries we often support

00:14:45.401 --> 00:14:47.050
a large swath of
the POSIX Standards.

00:14:47.051 --> 00:14:48.850
You can take common POSIX
libraries out there and

00:14:48.851 --> 00:14:51.720
bring them to the platform.

00:14:51.721 --> 00:14:55.720
We know the A7 compared to
something like an M4, AI,

00:14:55.721 --> 00:14:57.520
where we'll have a headroom
for the future for bringing

00:14:57.521 --> 00:15:01.000
new products to market
through this software update.

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:04.050
So what are these firewalls?

00:15:04.051 --> 00:15:05.220
When I say that term firewall,

00:15:05.221 --> 00:15:07.235
I don't mean a network firewall

00:15:07.236 --> 00:15:08.760
where you have a white list,

00:15:08.761 --> 00:15:10.390
and you're looking in
software, for example,

00:15:10.391 --> 00:15:12.180
a table of IP addresses
you're looking for.

00:15:12.181 --> 00:15:16.525
What I mean is that these
firewalls are like a mapping.

00:15:16.526 --> 00:15:18.600
This mapping is
done in registers,

00:15:18.601 --> 00:15:20.715
and when you can map a peripheral

00:15:20.716 --> 00:15:23.680
to a core, so in this example,

00:15:23.681 --> 00:15:25.550
we have multiple cores,

00:15:25.551 --> 00:15:28.090
the firewall guarantees that if

00:15:28.091 --> 00:15:29.340
another core tries to access

00:15:29.341 --> 00:15:30.610
that peripheral over the bus,

00:15:30.611 --> 00:15:31.980
that access is simply denied.

00:15:31.981 --> 00:15:33.645
It's just a no op.

00:15:33.646 --> 00:15:35.535
So being able to
program this firewalls,

00:15:35.536 --> 00:15:36.950
and having enforced in silicon,

00:15:36.951 --> 00:15:38.610
and having a "Deny
by Default" strategy

00:15:38.611 --> 00:15:40.480
means that you are
guarding against

00:15:40.481 --> 00:15:44.420
cross-cortex and cross-core
vulnerabilities.

00:15:44.421 --> 00:15:45.710
This applies to every resource in

00:15:45.711 --> 00:15:47.720
the system: Ram, network, flash,

00:15:47.721 --> 00:15:49.650
and every single
peripheral flows through

00:15:49.651 --> 00:15:52.870
this mapping in silicon.

00:15:52.871 --> 00:15:55.650
So this means if you're
on one of the cores,

00:15:55.651 --> 00:15:57.750
and a hacker attacks it and
they have access to yours,

00:15:57.751 --> 00:15:59.975
and they want to hop
over and access a spy,

00:15:59.976 --> 00:16:02.185
or access some of the SRAM
attached to the A7,

00:16:02.186 --> 00:16:04.185
it's simply not possible.

00:16:04.186 --> 00:16:06.290
Now, one of the interesting

00:16:06.291 --> 00:16:07.640
property theories
that we talked about,

00:16:07.641 --> 00:16:11.070
a lot of these silicon defenses

00:16:11.071 --> 00:16:12.520
are sticky, and what
does that mean?

00:16:12.521 --> 00:16:15.085
It means once you
program this mapping,

00:16:15.086 --> 00:16:17.290
it cannot be reset

00:16:17.291 --> 00:16:20.310
until the entire chip goes
through its reset process.

00:16:20.311 --> 00:16:22.390
So even if the code that programs

00:16:22.391 --> 00:16:26.330
the firewall is exploited or
has a privilege escalation,

00:16:26.331 --> 00:16:28.340
an attacker cannot reset

00:16:28.341 --> 00:16:29.790
the firewall and reprogram it for

00:16:29.791 --> 00:16:31.670
access to resources it
didn't originally have.

00:16:31.671 --> 00:16:35.400
Having that theme is
a write-once setting.

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:38.840
Finally, we have an MCU.
We have a traditional MCU

00:16:38.841 --> 00:16:42.160
on this Cross MCUs is where
you do real time computation.

00:16:42.161 --> 00:16:44.525
Real time access is
basically bare metal execution

00:16:44.526 --> 00:16:45.980
with a traditional Artas.

00:16:45.981 --> 00:16:48.255
Because of that
compartmentalization in silicon,

00:16:48.256 --> 00:16:51.190
we prevent that software from

00:16:51.191 --> 00:16:52.580
basically running amok other

00:16:52.581 --> 00:16:55.170
than the Sandbox we provided.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:00.320
So, basically, you can run
whatever you want there.

00:17:00.321 --> 00:17:02.030
A lot of manufacturers have

00:17:02.031 --> 00:17:03.895
their existing Artas
that they want to run,

00:17:03.896 --> 00:17:06.020
and they're free to
deploy it and run it,

00:17:06.021 --> 00:17:08.520
we just treat it
like an application.

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:11.350
So, we have a lot of
silicon partners that

00:17:11.351 --> 00:17:13.885
have made announcements as
supporting these chips.

00:17:13.886 --> 00:17:16.930
The first chips from MediaTek
is called the MT3620,

00:17:16.931 --> 00:17:18.615
it's available on market now.

00:17:18.616 --> 00:17:20.410
We're going to see a lot
more chips coming to

00:17:20.411 --> 00:17:23.780
market in this space over
the next couple of years.

00:17:23.830 --> 00:17:28.015
So, let's talk a little bit
about the operating system.

00:17:28.016 --> 00:17:30.454
At the bottom, we're
running our own processor,

00:17:30.455 --> 00:17:31.850
we're running in
trust zones and trust

00:17:31.851 --> 00:17:33.500
zone parlance, and secure world.

00:17:33.501 --> 00:17:35.130
We have a security monitor that

00:17:35.131 --> 00:17:37.100
protects access to resources,

00:17:37.101 --> 00:17:39.720
all those silicon countermeasures

00:17:39.721 --> 00:17:42.060
I talked about are all
programmed from Secure World,

00:17:42.061 --> 00:17:43.845
they can't be set
from normal world.

00:17:43.846 --> 00:17:45.615
There were Custom Linux kernel,

00:17:45.616 --> 00:17:47.860
these on-chip Cloud Services

00:17:47.861 --> 00:17:49.230
for authentication
and connectivity,

00:17:49.231 --> 00:17:50.440
and then you have

00:17:50.441 --> 00:17:52.540
our application called the
Containers or Sandboxes.

00:17:52.541 --> 00:17:54.085
Don't think about
a Docker Container.

00:17:54.086 --> 00:17:55.270
In the space we're talking

00:17:55.271 --> 00:17:56.460
about with a couple
of megs of RAM,

00:17:56.461 --> 00:17:59.710
a typical application
is maybe 80 kilobytes.

00:17:59.711 --> 00:18:01.580
So, we're talking about a Sandbox

00:18:01.581 --> 00:18:04.500
that's reduced the surface
area for execution.

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:08.310
So, little more detail here.

00:18:08.311 --> 00:18:09.400
So, as we mentioned earlier in

00:18:09.401 --> 00:18:11.180
this curated user environment
there's no passwords,

00:18:11.181 --> 00:18:12.620
no shell, no user accounts.

00:18:12.621 --> 00:18:15.290
Basically, application has
its own manifest format,

00:18:15.291 --> 00:18:17.350
the manifest is part of
the application package

00:18:17.351 --> 00:18:18.900
that's signed by Microsoft,

00:18:18.901 --> 00:18:20.340
we can parse that
manifest to determine

00:18:20.341 --> 00:18:22.794
the exact resources the
application's requesting,

00:18:22.795 --> 00:18:25.740
and guarantee that all those
resources are accessed.

00:18:25.741 --> 00:18:28.479
Of course, we use
things like TLS,

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:30.520
to implement
mutual authentication,

00:18:30.521 --> 00:18:32.430
those are run inside

00:18:32.431 --> 00:18:35.470
the operating system
in normal world.

00:18:35.471 --> 00:18:37.360
For Linux kernel has

00:18:37.361 --> 00:18:38.790
its own Linux
security module that

00:18:38.791 --> 00:18:40.840
protects resource acquisition and

00:18:40.841 --> 00:18:44.180
manages access to Pluton services

00:18:44.181 --> 00:18:45.950
like our random number generator,

00:18:45.951 --> 00:18:49.250
and the security monitor in
addition to granting access

00:18:49.251 --> 00:18:50.310
to hardware resources by

00:18:50.311 --> 00:18:52.260
programming all those
silicon countermeasures,

00:18:52.261 --> 00:18:55.585
also implements technologies
like erasure coding

00:18:55.586 --> 00:18:59.240
to guarantee against
runtime corruption on Flash.

00:18:59.241 --> 00:19:03.730
So, that's the OS, to jump
to the Security Service.

00:19:03.731 --> 00:19:05.690
What are we getting out
of the Security Service?

00:19:05.691 --> 00:19:09.185
How do we actually broker trust?

00:19:09.186 --> 00:19:11.810
Well, we use the
Security Service to

00:19:11.811 --> 00:19:13.090
connect all our devices and we

00:19:13.091 --> 00:19:14.950
send our failure
reporting through it,

00:19:14.951 --> 00:19:16.140
so we can do analytics and look

00:19:16.141 --> 00:19:17.725
for attacks out in the wild.

00:19:17.726 --> 00:19:19.320
We also use the
Security Service to push

00:19:19.321 --> 00:19:21.095
updates back down to devices,

00:19:21.096 --> 00:19:23.315
and Azure Sphere device updates
can't be turned off.

00:19:23.316 --> 00:19:24.600
It's part of the
agreement that device

00:19:24.601 --> 00:19:25.960
manufacturers except
that we're always

00:19:25.961 --> 00:19:29.365
allowed to take an update
and push it out the device.

00:19:29.366 --> 00:19:32.670
So, what are we getting
from attestation?

00:19:32.671 --> 00:19:34.550
So, a run attestation allows you

00:19:34.551 --> 00:19:38.510
to check not only the devices
running genuine software,

00:19:38.511 --> 00:19:39.940
but it's running up
to date software.

00:19:39.941 --> 00:19:41.755
So, where we're
leveraging attestation,

00:19:41.756 --> 00:19:43.420
is that an updated device

00:19:43.421 --> 00:19:45.190
is issued a very
short-lived cert.

00:19:45.191 --> 00:19:47.685
So, think about it a cert
that only lives a day.

00:19:47.686 --> 00:19:49.630
That cert then can be
presented to any service,

00:19:49.631 --> 00:19:51.900
when it's AWS, or Azure.

00:19:51.901 --> 00:19:54.055
If you can verify the cert chain,

00:19:54.056 --> 00:19:55.870
effectively Microsoft is
vouching for the health of

00:19:55.871 --> 00:19:57.320
the device for the moment

00:19:57.321 --> 00:19:59.240
in time when attestation
was completed.

00:19:59.241 --> 00:20:01.000
If a device is out of date,

00:20:01.001 --> 00:20:02.130
it doesn't get the cert
it actually gets

00:20:02.131 --> 00:20:03.330
something called a
quarantine cert,

00:20:03.331 --> 00:20:04.790
that can only be
used to connect to

00:20:04.791 --> 00:20:07.760
our update service and we can
force that update to occur.

00:20:07.761 --> 00:20:09.390
So, this allows us to prevent-

00:20:09.391 --> 00:20:10.720
basically allows us to take

00:20:10.721 --> 00:20:12.450
the security services
and scale out beyond

00:20:12.451 --> 00:20:14.320
Microsoft services by
using a certificate,

00:20:14.321 --> 00:20:16.190
which is really a

00:20:16.191 --> 00:20:18.750
common format that can
be understood in person.

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:25.085
So, of course, we use
Visual Studio, for development.

00:20:25.086 --> 00:20:27.755
You might be surprised a lot
of times in the MCU space,

00:20:27.756 --> 00:20:29.795
modern development tools
aren't very common.

00:20:29.796 --> 00:20:31.940
So, bringing a Visual Studio

00:20:31.941 --> 00:20:33.615
to market, I think,
there's a demo,

00:20:33.616 --> 00:20:35.610
I think tomorrow, so you guys

00:20:35.611 --> 00:20:37.635
can play with the device
and see how easy this is.

00:20:37.636 --> 00:20:39.390
Taking up the development kit,

00:20:39.391 --> 00:20:40.730
connects up to Azure and
send it to telemetry

00:20:40.731 --> 00:20:42.830
takes about 10 minutes.

00:20:42.831 --> 00:20:47.530
So, I'm running out of time.
So we're going to conclude.

00:20:47.531 --> 00:20:48.850
The other thing I want
to talk about we might

00:20:48.851 --> 00:20:50.560
not normally talk
about in this space,

00:20:50.561 --> 00:20:53.390
is we talk a lot about
subscriptions right now,

00:20:53.391 --> 00:20:54.505
subscription's a big way,

00:20:54.506 --> 00:20:56.445
a lot of companies do business,

00:20:56.446 --> 00:20:57.690
we didn't want to do that when it

00:20:57.691 --> 00:20:58.770
came to securing your device.

00:20:58.771 --> 00:21:00.575
We didn't want you to have
to think month to month,

00:21:00.576 --> 00:21:03.515
is this device worth
securing or not?

00:21:03.516 --> 00:21:05.445
So, when the chip is sold,

00:21:05.446 --> 00:21:06.865
it comes with a license,

00:21:06.866 --> 00:21:09.040
the license guarantees
the Security Service

00:21:09.041 --> 00:21:10.830
is going to run
basically for 10 years,

00:21:10.831 --> 00:21:11.980
we guarantee updates,

00:21:11.981 --> 00:21:13.940
we'll manage
the service, the device.

00:21:13.941 --> 00:21:16.700
So, that cost, I think,

00:21:16.701 --> 00:21:19.070
is where the public price
we've announced is

00:21:19.071 --> 00:21:22.460
less than $10 for 10 years,
including the chip.

00:21:22.461 --> 00:21:24.270
So, we're hoping to basically

00:21:24.271 --> 00:21:26.220
take some of the secure
service and push it

00:21:26.221 --> 00:21:28.250
down market into the MCU space

00:21:28.251 --> 00:21:30.180
with all the features we're
talking about in Pluton.

00:21:30.181 --> 00:21:34.210
The thing is that's open.

00:21:34.211 --> 00:21:38.010
So, we're licensing
the Pluton subsystem

00:21:38.011 --> 00:21:39.725
for free to any
silicon manufacturer

00:21:39.726 --> 00:21:41.815
that wants to implement
a sphere chip.

00:21:41.816 --> 00:21:43.870
We are, of course,

00:21:43.871 --> 00:21:45.090
it's Linux kernels GPL,

00:21:45.091 --> 00:21:48.275
so that's open and available
for people to contribute to,

00:21:48.276 --> 00:21:49.965
and it works with any Cloud.

00:21:49.966 --> 00:21:53.200
So, because the way this
Security Service works,

00:21:53.201 --> 00:21:55.655
if you're hosting all of
your service in WS, or Google,

00:21:55.656 --> 00:21:56.910
or Azure, you can

00:21:56.911 --> 00:21:58.610
leverage Security Service
for the security of

00:21:58.611 --> 00:22:02.640
the device and work with
any service that's out there.

00:22:02.641 --> 00:22:07.345
So, that is the 10,000
foot view Azure Sphere,

00:22:07.346 --> 00:22:10.090
and I'm happy to
take any questions.

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:19.000
&gt;&gt; Thanks for the talk.

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:22.455
It seems like updates
are critical,

00:22:22.456 --> 00:22:25.620
and there seems to be
a tension between what you

00:22:25.621 --> 00:22:28.795
allow to be updated and
what may not be updated.

00:22:28.796 --> 00:22:31.600
You mentioned the Firewall
can't be updated,

00:22:31.601 --> 00:22:34.180
and yet other things
can be updated.

00:22:34.181 --> 00:22:35.800
Obviously, if the firewall has

00:22:35.801 --> 00:22:37.755
a vulnerability and that
you'd want to update it,

00:22:37.756 --> 00:22:39.070
could you say a little bit

00:22:39.071 --> 00:22:42.080
about what's the philosophy

00:22:42.081 --> 00:22:44.285
and how you resolve the tension

00:22:44.286 --> 00:22:48.290
to ensure that updates
can't be abused

00:22:48.291 --> 00:22:52.910
and yet they will
occur when needed?

00:22:52.911 --> 00:22:55.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah, great question.
Basically, anything

00:22:55.951 --> 00:22:57.040
in software can be updated.

00:22:57.041 --> 00:23:01.615
So, the real tension is what
do we push into silicon?

00:23:01.616 --> 00:23:03.580
Is the functionality pushed into

00:23:03.581 --> 00:23:06.355
silicon either simple enough

00:23:06.356 --> 00:23:07.585
that we can have confidence
that it's correct?

00:23:07.586 --> 00:23:09.730
So a bug in the Firewall

00:23:09.731 --> 00:23:12.255
in silicon would
be not updatable.

00:23:12.256 --> 00:23:14.150
That would be a core vulnerability
in the chip that we'd

00:23:14.151 --> 00:23:15.070
effectively have to recall

00:23:15.071 --> 00:23:16.405
all the chips and replace them,

00:23:16.406 --> 00:23:18.910
or we try to use things
like pro verification.

00:23:18.911 --> 00:23:20.995
So portions of Pluton

00:23:20.996 --> 00:23:23.350
have been formally
verified to ensure that,

00:23:23.351 --> 00:23:26.070
for example, the cryptographic
algorithms are correct.

00:23:26.071 --> 00:23:28.450
If it's in software,
we're going to update it,

00:23:28.451 --> 00:23:29.830
we're going to ship
updates out to it.

00:23:29.831 --> 00:23:33.450
So, we try to draw that line
in terms of what we put

00:23:33.451 --> 00:23:34.990
to silicon and what we keep in

00:23:34.991 --> 00:23:37.925
software to determine
what's the deal.

00:23:37.926 --> 00:23:40.730
But, it's tough because if
you ship a bug and putting

00:23:40.731 --> 00:23:42.210
these cryptographic
algorithms in silicon

00:23:42.211 --> 00:23:43.975
means they're
already more secure,

00:23:43.976 --> 00:23:46.285
but it also means
if there's a bug,

00:23:46.286 --> 00:23:47.910
you're going to have to actually

00:23:47.911 --> 00:23:49.730
go out and replace the chip.

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.290
&gt;&gt; Great. Hello. Yes.
My name is Daniel.

00:23:53.291 --> 00:23:56.190
It sounds like this may

00:23:56.191 --> 00:23:59.035
incorporate the use of
a mini hardware security module.

00:23:59.036 --> 00:24:01.405
And if so,

00:24:01.406 --> 00:24:04.185
has it been submitted for
NIST validation and if so,

00:24:04.186 --> 00:24:06.605
what steps are we're
shooting for for it?

00:24:06.606 --> 00:24:08.580
&gt;&gt; Great. I don't
have all the details.

00:24:08.581 --> 00:24:10.825
So, internally, we're
FIPS compliant,

00:24:10.826 --> 00:24:12.760
and I think the team

00:24:12.761 --> 00:24:15.035
working- because I
didn't mention this,

00:24:15.036 --> 00:24:19.320
Pluton comes from a few decades

00:24:19.321 --> 00:24:20.740
of experience with
Microsoft hardware.

00:24:20.741 --> 00:24:23.765
So, this is internal silicon,
this isn't brand new.

00:24:23.766 --> 00:24:25.260
It's internal silicon
has been defending

00:24:25.261 --> 00:24:26.390
other products and
protecting other

00:24:26.391 --> 00:24:27.605
products in your ecosystem,

00:24:27.606 --> 00:24:29.150
that we've simplified a bit

00:24:29.151 --> 00:24:30.780
and brought down
into the MCU space.

00:24:30.781 --> 00:24:33.485
So, we've gone through
internally FIPS compliance.

00:24:33.486 --> 00:24:34.410
We've not gone through

00:24:34.411 --> 00:24:35.920
formal FIPS
certification but we get

00:24:35.921 --> 00:24:36.950
asked about that
a lot and I think

00:24:36.951 --> 00:24:38.490
that's going to be the next step.

00:24:38.491 --> 00:24:41.340
Yeah. I guess that I
forgot this one thing,

00:24:41.341 --> 00:24:43.170
I forgot to mention is that,

00:24:43.171 --> 00:24:46.200
you mentioned in HSM, Pluton

00:24:46.201 --> 00:24:47.200
just generates some key pairs

00:24:47.201 --> 00:24:48.675
internally in silicon as well.

00:24:48.676 --> 00:24:50.860
So, some of these chips

00:24:50.861 --> 00:24:52.630
where you blow fuses
and take keys,

00:24:52.631 --> 00:24:55.160
you do them in HSM, you
send them over wire,

00:24:55.161 --> 00:24:57.890
we even feel like
that's insecure.

00:24:57.891 --> 00:24:59.470
So, the keys are never
accessed by software,

00:24:59.471 --> 00:25:00.740
even the most trusted piece
of software in

00:25:00.741 --> 00:25:02.675
the device can never
see the private key.

00:25:02.676 --> 00:25:05.215
It's all generated in
silicon and blown in fuses.

00:25:05.216 --> 00:25:07.210
So, it was a good
question. Thank you.

00:25:07.211 --> 00:25:10.120
&gt;&gt; Hi, Subin Mohan from UIUC.

00:25:10.121 --> 00:25:12.500
Your motivation for this

00:25:12.501 --> 00:25:14.035
originally you talked
about MCUs, right?

00:25:14.036 --> 00:25:17.100
So, are there of
any real-time properties

00:25:17.101 --> 00:25:18.630
that can be guaranteed or

00:25:18.631 --> 00:25:20.410
real-time priorities in
the Linux OS that you

00:25:20.411 --> 00:25:23.430
have designed for this or
can we add it in later?

00:25:23.431 --> 00:25:25.620
&gt;&gt; I think you could look
at adding soft real-time,

00:25:25.621 --> 00:25:26.920
but it's one of
the reasons why we have

00:25:26.921 --> 00:25:28.660
that extra M4 Core.

00:25:28.661 --> 00:25:31.370
That M core that was
on that diagram,

00:25:31.371 --> 00:25:32.900
that's firewalled off is

00:25:32.901 --> 00:25:36.020
that basically the M Core
Bare-Metal execution.

00:25:36.021 --> 00:25:38.120
So, you can load
in RTOS, few RTOS,

00:25:38.121 --> 00:25:39.520
some other RTOS on there,

00:25:39.521 --> 00:25:41.015
and have full control
over the Core.

00:25:41.016 --> 00:25:42.665
Microsoft doesn't
interfere with it.

00:25:42.666 --> 00:25:44.440
So, if you needed to do

00:25:44.441 --> 00:25:45.880
microsecond timing or something

00:25:45.881 --> 00:25:46.730
like that you can do it there.

00:25:46.731 --> 00:25:51.640
So because of all the
connectivity operations

00:25:51.641 --> 00:25:53.930
and update the pieces that
go on in the Linux Kernel.

00:25:53.931 --> 00:25:55.270
It's not really the
place you want to do

00:25:55.271 --> 00:25:57.950
you real-time execution, yeah.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:01.720
&gt;&gt; Does it have
control over the MMU

00:26:01.721 --> 00:26:04.220
and memory management
as well for M Core?

00:26:04.221 --> 00:26:06.640
&gt;&gt; The M Core is not
MMU, it has an MPU.

00:26:06.641 --> 00:26:09.710
But it's given us some segments
in which to execute.

00:26:09.711 --> 00:26:10.970
And then within

00:26:10.971 --> 00:26:12.470
that space it's free to
do whatever it wants.

00:26:12.471 --> 00:26:14.230
So, it's free to take
that piece of RAM that it's

00:26:14.231 --> 00:26:16.730
given and treat it
however it likes.

00:26:16.731 --> 00:26:21.130
Any questions?. All right,

00:26:21.131 --> 00:26:22.360
great thanks if there
are other questions,

00:26:22.361 --> 00:26:22.815
please reach out to me,

00:26:22.816 --> 00:26:25.140
I'd love to talk
about it, thank you.

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:31.480
&gt;&gt; Hello, I'm Adam Chlipala and I

00:26:31.481 --> 00:26:33.570
am going to be
talking about some of

00:26:33.571 --> 00:26:36.650
our experiences applying
formal methods to systems

00:26:36.651 --> 00:26:40.050
and some ideas for- now
that formal methods are

00:26:40.051 --> 00:26:41.530
relatively practical
for this kind of

00:26:41.531 --> 00:26:43.320
stuff what does that
mean about how we should

00:26:43.321 --> 00:26:46.320
change the way that we
design our systems and take

00:26:46.321 --> 00:26:47.490
advantage of some of

00:26:47.491 --> 00:26:51.070
the structuring principles
that formal methods allow?

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:54.520
So, let me start out
by talking about maybe

00:26:54.521 --> 00:26:55.710
the standard story people

00:26:55.711 --> 00:26:56.830
have in their heads
about how we're going

00:26:56.831 --> 00:26:59.140
to use formal methods where
we're building systems,

00:26:59.141 --> 00:27:00.850
we somehow gather requirements

00:27:00.851 --> 00:27:01.920
for what those systems should do,

00:27:01.921 --> 00:27:04.450
we have designs, and
then we write some code,

00:27:04.451 --> 00:27:06.180
and we iterate through applying

00:27:06.181 --> 00:27:07.730
quality assurance methods like

00:27:07.731 --> 00:27:10.165
testing and debugging
and code review,

00:27:10.166 --> 00:27:11.990
and we do that enough, and we

00:27:11.991 --> 00:27:14.310
become sufficiently confident
that we can release

00:27:14.311 --> 00:27:16.050
something and let
people use it and not

00:27:16.051 --> 00:27:18.750
be too worried about
serious security problems.

00:27:18.751 --> 00:27:22.045
So, the standard story of
formal methods is that we

00:27:22.046 --> 00:27:25.810
have it as an alternative kind
of quality control that we

00:27:25.811 --> 00:27:28.290
apply without really changing

00:27:28.291 --> 00:27:31.280
the parts of the implementation
approach that would

00:27:31.281 --> 00:27:33.580
normally be used but we're
going to do some extra work to

00:27:33.581 --> 00:27:35.290
prove that the system

00:27:35.291 --> 00:27:37.200
really does what
it's supposed to do.

00:27:37.201 --> 00:27:40.060
In particular, especially
for my own work,

00:27:40.061 --> 00:27:42.280
what I'm talking about here is

00:27:42.281 --> 00:27:45.520
mechanized end-to-end proofs
of functional correctness.

00:27:45.521 --> 00:27:47.120
So, let me take a few slides to

00:27:47.121 --> 00:27:50.145
briefly summarize what
I mean by those words,

00:27:50.146 --> 00:27:51.760
and then I'll get into

00:27:51.761 --> 00:27:55.040
some more applications
of the approach.

00:27:55.041 --> 00:27:56.870
So when I say
mechanized proofs, I mean,

00:27:56.871 --> 00:27:59.520
we use a theorem prover
software package

00:27:59.521 --> 00:28:00.795
called Proof Assistant.

00:28:00.796 --> 00:28:03.200
One my group uses is called Coq,

00:28:03.201 --> 00:28:04.930
and what this means is you

00:28:04.931 --> 00:28:06.520
can take the actual
source code of

00:28:06.521 --> 00:28:08.710
your real system and write

00:28:08.711 --> 00:28:10.070
a formal specification for it

00:28:10.071 --> 00:28:12.060
as source code in
another language.

00:28:12.061 --> 00:28:14.800
Finally, write a proof as
source code and yet in

00:28:14.801 --> 00:28:16.330
another language that
establishes that

00:28:16.331 --> 00:28:18.270
your system really meets
the specification.

00:28:18.271 --> 00:28:19.630
We're going to feed all
of these three into

00:28:19.631 --> 00:28:22.055
the proof checking
software package and it will

00:28:22.056 --> 00:28:24.170
automatically validate
that the proof

00:28:24.171 --> 00:28:25.580
actually establishes

00:28:25.581 --> 00:28:28.280
the specification that
you have in mind.

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:31.630
When I talk about
functional correctness,

00:28:31.631 --> 00:28:32.900
I mean, we're not just going to,

00:28:32.901 --> 00:28:35.845
for instance, prove that
the system never crashes.

00:28:35.846 --> 00:28:37.930
Were going to write down a rather

00:28:37.931 --> 00:28:40.105
specific concept of correctness.

00:28:40.106 --> 00:28:41.930
For instance, if all the inputs

00:28:41.931 --> 00:28:43.510
up to this point
with a following,

00:28:43.511 --> 00:28:46.350
then the output it
better be this.

00:28:46.351 --> 00:28:49.720
Finally, when I talk about
end-to-end proofs, I mean,

00:28:49.721 --> 00:28:51.850
we have halfway realistic systems

00:28:51.851 --> 00:28:53.945
that are naturally
broken up into layers,

00:28:53.946 --> 00:28:56.035
and the layers all have
their own specifications

00:28:56.036 --> 00:28:58.550
and we can prove each layer
individually but

00:28:58.551 --> 00:29:00.130
then we take the time to plug

00:29:00.131 --> 00:29:03.025
all these results
together and get

00:29:03.026 --> 00:29:04.730
an end-to-end guarantee that only

00:29:04.731 --> 00:29:06.240
needs to talk about
sort of the top and

00:29:06.241 --> 00:29:09.895
bottom APIs of a stack like this.

00:29:09.896 --> 00:29:11.950
Many of the internal details

00:29:11.951 --> 00:29:15.130
drop out of the trusted
computing base.

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:19.540
So, these kinds of ideas can

00:29:19.541 --> 00:29:21.060
influence the ways that we

00:29:21.061 --> 00:29:23.185
address classic trade-offs
in system design,

00:29:23.186 --> 00:29:24.885
like the one between

00:29:24.886 --> 00:29:27.750
performance and maintainability
where usually we

00:29:27.751 --> 00:29:29.710
worried that as we improve
the performance of

00:29:29.711 --> 00:29:31.760
a system that the
code gets harder

00:29:31.761 --> 00:29:34.015
to understand but it's worth it

00:29:34.016 --> 00:29:37.915
given the all the use that we
expect our systems to see.

00:29:37.916 --> 00:29:42.275
So, one question that
we might have is,

00:29:42.276 --> 00:29:44.100
is this pattern
fundamental where today,

00:29:44.101 --> 00:29:47.735
we expect building a realistic
high-performance system

00:29:47.736 --> 00:29:49.335
involves writing a lot of

00:29:49.336 --> 00:29:51.100
intricate low-level code where

00:29:51.101 --> 00:29:52.870
it's very easy to make mistakes

00:29:52.871 --> 00:29:55.090
that jeopardize the correctness

00:29:55.091 --> 00:29:57.440
or the security of a system?

00:29:57.441 --> 00:29:59.950
So, let me try to

00:29:59.951 --> 00:30:02.170
convince you that we can use
formal methods to actually

00:30:02.171 --> 00:30:04.390
change this assumption
for building

00:30:04.391 --> 00:30:07.735
realistic systems and
I'll do that with a,

00:30:07.736 --> 00:30:09.950
just start out with
a concrete example that we've

00:30:09.951 --> 00:30:12.650
already explored at MIT.

00:30:12.651 --> 00:30:16.300
So, one area which was
mentioned as just part of

00:30:16.301 --> 00:30:18.055
the story in the last talk

00:30:18.056 --> 00:30:21.010
is cryptographic primitives,
and in particular,

00:30:21.011 --> 00:30:23.690
elliptic curve
cryptography is one of

00:30:23.691 --> 00:30:27.020
the most widely
used approaches for

00:30:27.021 --> 00:30:30.890
instance for initiating
an HTTPS connection.

00:30:30.891 --> 00:30:32.930
Here's some C code written

00:30:32.931 --> 00:30:34.975
by one of the experts
on this kind of stuff

00:30:34.976 --> 00:30:37.660
for the elliptic curve that
is generally viewed as

00:30:37.661 --> 00:30:40.355
the easiest one to
implement efficiently.

00:30:40.356 --> 00:30:42.180
I think this is code for

00:30:42.181 --> 00:30:45.170
taking a big number
that's too big to fit in

00:30:45.171 --> 00:30:47.420
one register of
hardware and squaring

00:30:47.421 --> 00:30:51.365
it Modulo some
large prime number.

00:30:51.366 --> 00:30:54.015
It is moderately intricate

00:30:54.016 --> 00:30:55.985
and it's repetitive
enough that if you try to

00:30:55.986 --> 00:30:58.370
audit this you might
easily lure yourself into

00:30:58.371 --> 00:30:59.890
a false sense of security

00:30:59.891 --> 00:31:01.645
and believe everything
looks okay.

00:31:01.646 --> 00:31:05.020
This is just one part of
a larger piece of code

00:31:05.021 --> 00:31:07.000
which historically this kind of

00:31:07.001 --> 00:31:09.925
thing has all been
written by hand.

00:31:09.926 --> 00:31:12.770
But it'll be nice to know
this code is correct and

00:31:12.771 --> 00:31:15.720
there are few competing
approaches to doing that,

00:31:15.721 --> 00:31:18.185
one of them is to find

00:31:18.186 --> 00:31:20.300
a student or an intern
who's willing to read

00:31:20.301 --> 00:31:21.660
through all these
lines of code and

00:31:21.661 --> 00:31:23.070
write lots of invariance and

00:31:23.071 --> 00:31:26.220
convince an SMT solver
that this is correct.

00:31:26.221 --> 00:31:28.520
That's a wonderful
time-honored technique

00:31:28.521 --> 00:31:30.435
and I won't try to
trash talk too much.

00:31:30.436 --> 00:31:32.610
But what we could do
instead is wonder,

00:31:32.611 --> 00:31:34.120
do we really need
to write code like

00:31:34.121 --> 00:31:35.955
this to get
high-performance crypto?

00:31:35.956 --> 00:31:38.330
Maybe it's an opportunity to

00:31:38.331 --> 00:31:42.475
notice building formal proofs
of things is hard,

00:31:42.476 --> 00:31:44.280
it pushes us towards simplifying

00:31:44.281 --> 00:31:45.550
system implementations which is

00:31:45.551 --> 00:31:47.090
an independently useful thing.

00:31:47.091 --> 00:31:49.380
Maybe there's a better way
to write this where

00:31:49.381 --> 00:31:50.875
we'll get a proof

00:31:50.876 --> 00:31:53.215
and we won't have to
look at code like this.

00:31:53.216 --> 00:31:55.300
So, just to motivate
a little bit more about

00:31:55.301 --> 00:31:57.250
why this is
an interesting domain?

00:31:57.251 --> 00:31:58.920
It is the standard devices

00:31:58.921 --> 00:32:00.575
don't write your own
crypto code and

00:32:00.576 --> 00:32:04.300
probably most developers
try to follow this advice,

00:32:04.301 --> 00:32:04.940
most of the time,

00:32:04.941 --> 00:32:06.770
we have a relatively
small number of libraries

00:32:06.771 --> 00:32:09.990
that implement the most important
cryptographic routines.

00:32:09.991 --> 00:32:13.130
But there's a
moderately large space

00:32:13.131 --> 00:32:14.560
out there of
different cryptographic

00:32:14.561 --> 00:32:16.195
algorithms that matter.

00:32:16.196 --> 00:32:19.340
Then there are parameters
for those algorithms,

00:32:19.341 --> 00:32:22.190
one important example is
a large prime number that

00:32:22.191 --> 00:32:24.660
you use as the modulus
for arithmetic and

00:32:24.661 --> 00:32:27.465
then there's
a small-to-medium set of

00:32:27.466 --> 00:32:29.705
interestingly different
hardware architectures

00:32:29.706 --> 00:32:31.310
that we want to run this code on.

00:32:31.311 --> 00:32:33.645
When I was first learning
about this area,

00:32:33.646 --> 00:32:35.940
I was really surprised
to hear that

00:32:35.941 --> 00:32:38.530
for every element of
this three-way cross product,

00:32:38.531 --> 00:32:41.130
the state of practice is to
rewrite all the code from

00:32:41.131 --> 00:32:43.065
scratch sometimes an assembly

00:32:43.066 --> 00:32:44.780
to get the best performance.

00:32:44.781 --> 00:32:47.055
So, there really isn't,

00:32:47.056 --> 00:32:49.340
there haven't been
very good abstraction in

00:32:49.341 --> 00:32:50.525
the way that this kind of code

00:32:50.526 --> 00:32:52.305
is developed and maintained.

00:32:52.306 --> 00:32:53.940
That means there are lot of

00:32:53.941 --> 00:32:56.050
opportunities for making
mistakes where we get

00:32:56.051 --> 00:32:58.330
incorrect cryptographic
primitives that

00:32:58.331 --> 00:33:01.540
can wreck your
security guarantees.

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:04.885
So we wanted to go at this

00:33:04.886 --> 00:33:07.750
in the so-called
"Correct-by-Construction" way.

00:33:07.751 --> 00:33:10.510
It'll be a way of
writing the code so at

00:33:10.511 --> 00:33:12.270
every point all the
code you have is

00:33:12.271 --> 00:33:14.795
guaranteed to be computing
the correct answer.

00:33:14.796 --> 00:33:16.980
And there's a long history
of this kind of

00:33:16.981 --> 00:33:18.730
formal reasoning and cryptography

00:33:18.731 --> 00:33:20.530
where we typically start
with a property like,

00:33:20.531 --> 00:33:22.540
I have a digital signature
scheme- no one

00:33:22.541 --> 00:33:23.570
can forge signatures if they

00:33:23.571 --> 00:33:24.990
don't know the right secrets.

00:33:24.991 --> 00:33:26.535
And then we can apply

00:33:26.536 --> 00:33:29.190
the idea of protocol
verification to show that

00:33:29.191 --> 00:33:31.380
some mathematical
algorithm provides

00:33:31.381 --> 00:33:34.520
that guarantee subject
the standard assumptions.

00:33:34.521 --> 00:33:36.830
And then we could
do, for instance,

00:33:36.831 --> 00:33:39.970
implementations synthesis
to come up with

00:33:39.971 --> 00:33:42.375
low-level fast code that

00:33:42.376 --> 00:33:44.205
provably implements
that algorithm.

00:33:44.206 --> 00:33:46.860
I think the next talk in
this session we'll talk about

00:33:46.861 --> 00:33:48.120
the other direction
implementation

00:33:48.121 --> 00:33:49.280
verification where you have

00:33:49.281 --> 00:33:50.960
the code already and
then you're going

00:33:50.961 --> 00:33:53.030
to prove that it
implements the algorithm.

00:33:53.031 --> 00:33:55.390
But let me talk about
the downward direction,

00:33:55.391 --> 00:33:56.190
the way I've drawn this here.

00:33:56.191 --> 00:33:57.900
Implementation synthesis
is what I'll be

00:33:57.901 --> 00:34:00.425
focusing on in this project.

00:34:00.426 --> 00:34:02.510
So to give an idea
of how we can in

00:34:02.511 --> 00:34:04.210
a principled way derive

00:34:04.211 --> 00:34:05.990
the code that we want to run
instead of just starting

00:34:05.991 --> 00:34:07.985
with a pile of C
code or assembly.

00:34:07.986 --> 00:34:09.580
We'll start out with mathematical

00:34:09.581 --> 00:34:11.280
algorithms which
in this domain of

00:34:11.281 --> 00:34:14.955
elliptic curves are all about
two-dimensional geometry.

00:34:14.956 --> 00:34:18.399
And then typically
one substitutes

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:20.220
in a less obvious but more

00:34:20.221 --> 00:34:22.130
efficient implementation
of points

00:34:22.131 --> 00:34:24.970
which counter-intuitively
adds more coordinates

00:34:24.971 --> 00:34:26.160
to make things faster because

00:34:26.161 --> 00:34:28.790
we can run fewer
expensive instructions

00:34:28.791 --> 00:34:30.680
when we rearrange
things this way.

00:34:30.681 --> 00:34:32.310
And so this first step is

00:34:32.311 --> 00:34:36.000
just a classic data structure
correctness proof in

00:34:36.001 --> 00:34:37.690
the sense of abstract data types

00:34:37.691 --> 00:34:39.975
and abstraction relations
and all that stuff.

00:34:39.976 --> 00:34:42.430
So we write down an
abstraction relation

00:34:42.431 --> 00:34:43.770
between these two versions and

00:34:43.771 --> 00:34:45.940
prove that it's
respected by the code.

00:34:45.941 --> 00:34:48.830
Then we have to implement
the individual coordinates

00:34:48.831 --> 00:34:51.050
of these points which
are by themselves pretty

00:34:51.051 --> 00:34:52.430
hard to get to run quickly

00:34:52.431 --> 00:34:54.660
because every coordinate
is way too large to fit

00:34:54.661 --> 00:34:58.700
in the registers that
a commodity hardware provides.

00:34:58.701 --> 00:35:00.480
So we have to think about
division of numbers into

00:35:00.481 --> 00:35:03.845
multiple digits and so we

00:35:03.846 --> 00:35:09.620
prove big arithmetic in
this style to connect that part.

00:35:09.621 --> 00:35:11.600
And then finally, an aspect

00:35:11.601 --> 00:35:14.210
that I won't have time to talk
about in too much detail,

00:35:14.211 --> 00:35:16.650
we have a verified compiler at

00:35:16.651 --> 00:35:17.790
the end that takes

00:35:17.791 --> 00:35:19.320
the functional programs
that are coming out

00:35:19.321 --> 00:35:20.990
of this process that work

00:35:20.991 --> 00:35:22.860
with infinite precision integers

00:35:22.861 --> 00:35:26.110
and infers BitWits for

00:35:26.111 --> 00:35:27.500
all temporary variables and does

00:35:27.501 --> 00:35:29.275
other compiler
optimization things.

00:35:29.276 --> 00:35:30.665
And then out pops

00:35:30.666 --> 00:35:32.280
idiomatic C code with

00:35:32.281 --> 00:35:33.300
particular types for

00:35:33.301 --> 00:35:34.895
all the variables
that are being used.

00:35:34.896 --> 00:35:36.350
The important thing is
each of those arrows

00:35:36.351 --> 00:35:37.450
on the right side is carrying

00:35:37.451 --> 00:35:38.810
a proof and they're all snapped

00:35:38.811 --> 00:35:40.400
together in a common framework.

00:35:40.401 --> 00:35:42.900
So we get that the
low level code is truly

00:35:42.901 --> 00:35:45.430
implementing the
mathematical algorithm

00:35:45.431 --> 00:35:46.405
that we started with.

00:35:46.406 --> 00:35:48.900
And this whole pipeline
runs automatically.

00:35:48.901 --> 00:35:53.890
It's not a new manual effort
to consider a new space.

00:35:53.891 --> 00:35:55.455
I showed you three clouds.

00:35:55.456 --> 00:35:56.680
When you change up one of

00:35:56.681 --> 00:35:58.340
the second two clouds
this is automatic,

00:35:58.341 --> 00:36:00.750
when you have a new algorithm
that's new work.

00:36:01.590 --> 00:36:05.335
So our project is called
Fiat Cryptography.

00:36:05.336 --> 00:36:07.110
It takes place within
the context of

00:36:07.111 --> 00:36:08.890
a larger multi-university project

00:36:08.891 --> 00:36:11.320
called Science of
Digg Specifications,

00:36:11.321 --> 00:36:13.355
which paid for this lovely logo.

00:36:13.356 --> 00:36:14.910
Which is reference
to the spy versus

00:36:14.911 --> 00:36:18.045
spy comics for anyone who
is familiar with those.

00:36:18.046 --> 00:36:19.940
And I've listed down
here my students

00:36:19.941 --> 00:36:21.430
who've worked on this project.

00:36:21.431 --> 00:36:23.150
This is basically a platform

00:36:23.151 --> 00:36:25.090
for Correct-by-Construction
generation of

00:36:25.091 --> 00:36:27.295
elliptic curve cryptography
implementations

00:36:27.296 --> 00:36:30.040
and we hope later
other kinds of cryptography.

00:36:30.041 --> 00:36:33.630
So what we basically
do is we're going

00:36:33.631 --> 00:36:36.020
to think about the arithmetic

00:36:36.021 --> 00:36:38.480
that goes into these algorithms
to a high level.

00:36:38.481 --> 00:36:39.350
We're going to do

00:36:39.351 --> 00:36:40.970
some functional programming
of the kind that you

00:36:40.971 --> 00:36:43.475
might even assign at
a freshman programming class.

00:36:43.476 --> 00:36:45.620
We're going to
represent big numbers

00:36:45.621 --> 00:36:47.760
as lists of pairs of numbers.

00:36:47.761 --> 00:36:51.945
Every pair has a compile-time
part and a run-time part.

00:36:51.946 --> 00:36:54.315
The compile-time part is
the weight of that digit,

00:36:54.316 --> 00:36:55.990
It's like how many bits
to shift it by.

00:36:55.991 --> 00:36:58.785
The run-time part is
the actual digit value.

00:36:58.786 --> 00:37:00.450
We're not going to be very

00:37:00.451 --> 00:37:02.440
specific about which
weights are allowed,

00:37:02.441 --> 00:37:03.410
whether we can have duplicate

00:37:03.411 --> 00:37:04.510
weights or anything like that.

00:37:04.511 --> 00:37:06.890
We just have lists and pairs
of numbers and we can write

00:37:06.891 --> 00:37:07.950
down implementations of

00:37:07.951 --> 00:37:10.300
classic operations
like multiplication.

00:37:10.301 --> 00:37:12.030
That one works by just taking

00:37:12.031 --> 00:37:15.175
all the cross products of
the elements of two lists.

00:37:15.176 --> 00:37:16.740
And we can just write that as a

00:37:16.741 --> 00:37:18.294
functional program that uses

00:37:18.295 --> 00:37:21.305
famous higher-order functions
like Flatmap and Map.

00:37:21.306 --> 00:37:23.665
And it's a very small
amount of code,

00:37:23.666 --> 00:37:25.780
and we can write lemmas that we

00:37:25.781 --> 00:37:28.430
prove in the coke theorem
prove that shows that

00:37:28.431 --> 00:37:31.220
this multiplication
operation is related to

00:37:31.221 --> 00:37:33.140
the normal
multiplication operator

00:37:33.141 --> 00:37:35.460
in the appropriate way.

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:38.750
Then we can take ingredients
like that and put them

00:37:38.751 --> 00:37:41.290
together into
higher level operations.

00:37:41.291 --> 00:37:43.260
So this is one, that is

00:37:43.261 --> 00:37:45.450
going to take in
two big numbers A and

00:37:45.451 --> 00:37:48.970
B that follow a particular
digit representation

00:37:48.971 --> 00:37:50.470
with n digits in this case.

00:37:50.471 --> 00:37:52.000
This function is
actually generic in the

00:37:52.001 --> 00:37:54.605
number n using polymorphism.

00:37:54.606 --> 00:37:56.290
And then we convert each of

00:37:56.291 --> 00:37:58.700
those fixed representations into

00:37:58.701 --> 00:38:00.975
our flexible internal
representation.

00:38:00.976 --> 00:38:02.250
We do the multiplication,

00:38:02.251 --> 00:38:04.120
we run a mod operation which is

00:38:04.121 --> 00:38:06.380
by convention called
Reduce here and

00:38:06.381 --> 00:38:07.990
then we convert back out of

00:38:07.991 --> 00:38:10.020
the flexible representation into

00:38:10.021 --> 00:38:12.760
the fixed network format.

00:38:12.761 --> 00:38:14.790
So this is a really nice from

00:38:14.791 --> 00:38:16.310
a software engineering
standpoint..

00:38:16.311 --> 00:38:18.159
This code is so much
easier to understand

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:20.515
than the C code that
I showed before.

00:38:20.516 --> 00:38:22.040
But if we just say,

00:38:22.041 --> 00:38:23.830
take this and treat
it like OCaml code or

00:38:23.831 --> 00:38:25.860
Haskell code and compile
it and normal way

00:38:25.861 --> 00:38:27.910
it's like one 100 or more

00:38:27.911 --> 00:38:30.525
times slower than that
C code that I showed.

00:38:30.526 --> 00:38:32.515
So we need to do
something about that.

00:38:32.516 --> 00:38:35.165
And the approach we're
going to take is

00:38:35.166 --> 00:38:38.150
a classic idea in compilers
called Partial Evaluation.

00:38:38.151 --> 00:38:40.730
So we have for instance,
our multiply operation it's

00:38:40.731 --> 00:38:44.250
parametrized on
the a static part.

00:38:44.251 --> 00:38:47.000
Inflammation on
the digit representation

00:38:47.001 --> 00:38:48.040
of numbers which could be

00:38:48.041 --> 00:38:49.570
different for
different elliptic curves,

00:38:49.571 --> 00:38:51.340
and then we have
the actual digit values.

00:38:51.341 --> 00:38:53.880
So first, we'll specialize

00:38:53.881 --> 00:38:57.620
this function to a particular
set of digit bit widths,

00:38:57.621 --> 00:39:00.050
now all it takes in are
the digits themselves and

00:39:00.051 --> 00:39:02.900
then we will reduce and simplify

00:39:02.901 --> 00:39:04.980
algebraically that program until

00:39:04.981 --> 00:39:09.290
all the overhead of a generisity
is gone and we have

00:39:09.291 --> 00:39:12.470
a lean specialized
multiply routine

00:39:12.471 --> 00:39:13.780
and then we'll actually

00:39:13.781 --> 00:39:15.150
feed this into
the verified compiler

00:39:15.151 --> 00:39:16.860
I talked about which will infer

00:39:16.861 --> 00:39:18.680
bit widths and do it
the other thing is you

00:39:18.681 --> 00:39:19.730
need to get C code from

00:39:19.731 --> 00:39:23.235
the functional programming
view that we started with.

00:39:23.236 --> 00:39:26.550
So how does this actually
pay off for performance?

00:39:26.551 --> 00:39:29.075
So we, oh I should mention,

00:39:29.076 --> 00:39:30.500
each of these arrows here is

00:39:30.501 --> 00:39:33.380
a built-in operation of
our chosen theorem Prover Cox.

00:39:33.381 --> 00:39:36.655
So this is all very
straightforward to do.

00:39:36.656 --> 00:39:39.300
Specialization is just partially

00:39:39.301 --> 00:39:41.930
applying a curried function
to some of its arguments and

00:39:41.931 --> 00:39:45.315
the reduce stage is just
calling a built-in reducer that

00:39:45.316 --> 00:39:47.150
is one of the standard
building blocks

00:39:47.151 --> 00:39:49.065
of proof of program correctness.

00:39:49.066 --> 00:39:51.085
So that's a very natural process.

00:39:51.086 --> 00:39:53.000
So we get our C code
that looks a lot

00:39:53.001 --> 00:39:56.175
like the code I showed
at the beginning,

00:39:56.176 --> 00:39:59.020
at least at the abstract
syntax level.

00:39:59.021 --> 00:40:00.660
It's not as nice to
read directly in

00:40:00.661 --> 00:40:02.775
the concrete characters
that we output.

00:40:02.776 --> 00:40:04.910
And if we can compare
performance on curve

00:40:04.911 --> 00:40:07.900
25519 which is the for instance,

00:40:07.901 --> 00:40:10.100
Google wants to talk
this curve to you if you

00:40:10.101 --> 00:40:12.365
try to connect to
their main website.

00:40:12.366 --> 00:40:14.415
If your browser supports
it'll use this one.

00:40:14.416 --> 00:40:19.350
And here's some examples
of how many CPU cycles are

00:40:19.351 --> 00:40:21.430
needed for one of

00:40:21.431 --> 00:40:22.730
the top level elliptic

00:40:22.731 --> 00:40:24.520
curve operations I
forget which one.

00:40:24.521 --> 00:40:29.020
And we can see there's
our code up at the top there.

00:40:29.021 --> 00:40:31.830
We generate both 64-bit
and 32-bit versions.

00:40:31.831 --> 00:40:34.660
So these benchmark results
are run on a 64-bit machine,

00:40:34.661 --> 00:40:35.985
so the 32-bit results aren't

00:40:35.986 --> 00:40:37.595
super interesting
for performance.

00:40:37.596 --> 00:40:38.940
And we're very close to

00:40:38.941 --> 00:40:43.420
the best known assembly code
for this elliptic curve.

00:40:43.421 --> 00:40:45.700
And in fact we are now as far

00:40:45.701 --> 00:40:48.395
as I know generating
the fastest known C code for

00:40:48.396 --> 00:40:49.950
essentially every
elliptic curve with

00:40:49.951 --> 00:40:53.705
one automatic pipeline
that generates proofs.

00:40:53.706 --> 00:40:58.910
And to demonstrate that
we scraped Information on

00:40:58.911 --> 00:41:01.480
elliptic curves online and
tried to compute the set of

00:41:01.481 --> 00:41:02.900
all the prime numbers that

00:41:02.901 --> 00:41:04.830
anyone associates
with this field.

00:41:04.831 --> 00:41:07.210
So we can automatically
generate implementations

00:41:07.211 --> 00:41:10.970
for modular arithmetic
for all those primes.

00:41:10.971 --> 00:41:14.780
And we did it for
both 64-bit desktop machine with

00:41:14.781 --> 00:41:15.980
an X86 processor and

00:41:15.981 --> 00:41:19.120
a 32-bit arm machine which is
actually an Android phone,

00:41:19.121 --> 00:41:22.300
and we compare against
the previous highest performance

00:41:22.301 --> 00:41:24.975
generic implementation
of this stuff

00:41:24.976 --> 00:41:28.290
which is the new multi
precision library

00:41:28.291 --> 00:41:30.730
which has multiple
variants one of

00:41:30.731 --> 00:41:33.970
which actually is appropriate for

00:41:33.971 --> 00:41:36.810
secure crypto-style
applications where you don't

00:41:36.811 --> 00:41:38.310
leak information about inputs

00:41:38.311 --> 00:41:40.300
to your arithmetic
through timing.

00:41:40.301 --> 00:41:42.320
But we also compare it
against the version

00:41:42.321 --> 00:41:43.960
that does leak information
through timing

00:41:43.961 --> 00:41:45.550
which should only make it

00:41:45.551 --> 00:41:47.815
harder for us to show
performance improvement.

00:41:47.816 --> 00:41:50.250
And still over the space of

00:41:50.251 --> 00:41:53.670
all these primes that we
spidered off the internet.

00:41:53.671 --> 00:41:55.575
We were seeing typically

00:41:55.576 --> 00:41:58.875
a performance improvement
in the range of 2X to

00:41:58.876 --> 00:42:01.545
10X and we have formal proofs of

00:42:01.546 --> 00:42:02.750
all these implementations which

00:42:02.751 --> 00:42:06.000
the multi precision
library doesn't have.

00:42:06.410 --> 00:42:10.115
And finally we
managed to convince

00:42:10.116 --> 00:42:13.720
the team at Google that
manages the boring SSL library

00:42:13.721 --> 00:42:16.140
which is a started out as

00:42:16.141 --> 00:42:19.910
a heavily inspired by the
OpenSSL library but as

00:42:19.911 --> 00:42:21.480
diverged a lot since then to

00:42:21.481 --> 00:42:23.060
adopt our approach to replace

00:42:23.061 --> 00:42:24.790
their grungy handwritten code

00:42:24.791 --> 00:42:26.770
for elliptic curve arithmetic.

00:42:26.771 --> 00:42:29.520
So the two most commonly used
elliptic curves are curve

00:42:29.521 --> 00:42:33.590
25519 and P256 and
our code is in there now

00:42:33.591 --> 00:42:35.060
for both of those with distinct

00:42:35.061 --> 00:42:37.125
64-bit and 32-bit versions

00:42:37.126 --> 00:42:40.120
and those student doing
an internship as we speak at

00:42:40.121 --> 00:42:44.100
Google who will hopefully
have P384 the third most

00:42:44.101 --> 00:42:45.480
frequently used elliptic curve

00:42:45.481 --> 00:42:48.870
done in the next month or so.

00:42:48.871 --> 00:42:50.770
And so this library

00:42:50.771 --> 00:42:52.440
is used in Chrome,
it's used in Android,

00:42:52.441 --> 00:42:54.100
it's used on Google servers and

00:42:54.101 --> 00:42:57.240
so now about half of all
HTTPS connections from

00:42:57.241 --> 00:42:59.860
browsers use are formally

00:42:59.861 --> 00:43:03.950
generated implementations of
[inaudible] curve operations.

00:43:05.550 --> 00:43:10.260
So, I started out
posing this question,

00:43:10.261 --> 00:43:13.310
do we really need to
spend our time writing

00:43:13.311 --> 00:43:15.305
intricate low-level code to get

00:43:15.306 --> 00:43:18.390
performance in the systems
that we're building?

00:43:18.391 --> 00:43:20.500
For talking about formal methods,

00:43:20.501 --> 00:43:23.090
it's probably not a surprise
that we can, to some extent,

00:43:23.091 --> 00:43:25.365
get rid of that bug
prone quality,

00:43:25.366 --> 00:43:28.410
even if we keep writing the
same kinds of code as before.

00:43:28.411 --> 00:43:30.480
But hopefully, I've
also showed how we can

00:43:30.481 --> 00:43:33.240
remove the need to write
low-level code and do more

00:43:33.241 --> 00:43:36.050
of our thinking in a nice
high-level way with more of

00:43:36.051 --> 00:43:37.930
the nice software
engineering properties

00:43:37.931 --> 00:43:40.640
that we prefer to have.

00:43:40.641 --> 00:43:43.590
Here's a related question.

00:43:43.591 --> 00:43:47.610
Whenever we add new
abstractions to a system,

00:43:47.611 --> 00:43:49.010
we worry that there will
be some performance

00:43:49.011 --> 00:43:50.370
cost for those abstractions,

00:43:50.371 --> 00:43:53.800
even if it makes the system
nicer to think about.

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:58.360
If we do adopt these kinds of
end-to-end formal methods,

00:43:58.361 --> 00:44:03.410
there could be ways to
lower the costs of adding

00:44:03.411 --> 00:44:06.530
abstraction to
a system that we might

00:44:06.531 --> 00:44:07.830
not think about if we have to

00:44:07.831 --> 00:44:09.830
stick to traditional
implementations.

00:44:09.831 --> 00:44:13.890
So, let me talk

00:44:13.891 --> 00:44:15.730
about one way to think

00:44:15.731 --> 00:44:18.220
about how we could apply
this idea in general.

00:44:18.221 --> 00:44:20.970
I think the last talk
was a great example of

00:44:20.971 --> 00:44:24.500
a classic approach to
security and isolation

00:44:24.501 --> 00:44:28.149
in a system by imposing
various runtime mechanisms to

00:44:28.150 --> 00:44:30.150
contain compartments
and limit what could

00:44:30.151 --> 00:44:33.005
happen to different
parts of the system.

00:44:33.006 --> 00:44:35.860
I think of a lot of different
examples from processors,

00:44:35.861 --> 00:44:37.780
to operating systems,
to database systems,

00:44:37.781 --> 00:44:41.920
as having their missions in
life are to provide a kind of

00:44:41.921 --> 00:44:43.870
high-level imaginary view of

00:44:43.871 --> 00:44:47.650
how a bunch of parts of a system
run and share resources.

00:44:47.651 --> 00:44:48.510
Where we can imagine there are

00:44:48.511 --> 00:44:50.745
these impenetrable walls between

00:44:50.746 --> 00:44:54.335
the different sub-parts
of a world like this.

00:44:54.336 --> 00:44:56.380
Every one of the parts
gets to be written in

00:44:56.381 --> 00:44:59.320
a nice high-level language,
uses nice APIs,

00:44:59.321 --> 00:45:02.355
we can pretend there are
dedicated processing,

00:45:02.356 --> 00:45:04.470
resources, dedicated
storage, and so on,

00:45:04.471 --> 00:45:06.425
for each one of these blocks.

00:45:06.426 --> 00:45:10.069
Many traditional deployed
systems today literally

00:45:10.070 --> 00:45:11.700
realized this picture and the way

00:45:11.701 --> 00:45:14.590
hardware and software are set up.

00:45:14.591 --> 00:45:15.810
But in general, what

00:45:15.811 --> 00:45:16.950
we're trying to do
here is come up with

00:45:16.951 --> 00:45:20.780
a real optimized system
that in some sense

00:45:20.781 --> 00:45:22.080
can be having a dream and

00:45:22.081 --> 00:45:24.090
imagining it lives in
that nice world but there's

00:45:24.091 --> 00:45:25.650
a lot more resource sharing to

00:45:25.651 --> 00:45:28.170
improve performance
in various ways.

00:45:28.171 --> 00:45:31.955
We might call this top view
of things virtual machine,

00:45:31.956 --> 00:45:34.915
maybe a kind of
older sense of the phrase,

00:45:34.916 --> 00:45:39.260
which is just a nice
abstract view of

00:45:39.261 --> 00:45:41.270
an executing system that
doesn't have to get into

00:45:41.271 --> 00:45:44.035
the details of how we
do resource sharing.

00:45:44.036 --> 00:45:46.480
Or we could just call it a
specification which is already

00:45:46.481 --> 00:45:49.250
a common idea in formal methods,

00:45:49.251 --> 00:45:51.690
and think about how we can use

00:45:51.691 --> 00:45:54.900
compile-time reasoning to
show that a system truly

00:45:54.901 --> 00:45:56.690
implements this
abstraction without

00:45:56.691 --> 00:45:59.540
leaving any runtime residue
of the approach that

00:45:59.541 --> 00:46:01.800
forces us to keep paying
a performance cost

00:46:01.801 --> 00:46:05.280
each time we use the mechanisms
that we develop.

00:46:05.281 --> 00:46:09.200
So, one direction
that I'm interested

00:46:09.201 --> 00:46:13.420
in pushing on in
the near future is to try to

00:46:13.421 --> 00:46:15.790
take this all the way

00:46:15.791 --> 00:46:18.100
and move all of

00:46:18.101 --> 00:46:21.340
our reasoning about
isolation to compile time,

00:46:21.341 --> 00:46:23.250
and get good performance,

00:46:23.251 --> 00:46:24.930
get good abstractions
without having

00:46:24.931 --> 00:46:26.860
to make compromises there.

00:46:26.861 --> 00:46:29.370
So, the idea is,

00:46:29.371 --> 00:46:32.810
I wanted to think of a stack
of systems components as

00:46:32.811 --> 00:46:34.920
essentially a platform for

00:46:34.921 --> 00:46:37.395
efficient execution of
simple functional programs,

00:46:37.396 --> 00:46:40.070
where we build up abstractions
that successively

00:46:40.071 --> 00:46:41.370
show how to implement
the high-level

00:46:41.371 --> 00:46:42.925
features that we're relying on.

00:46:42.926 --> 00:46:45.260
I want the starting
specifications

00:46:45.261 --> 00:46:46.440
we work with to be so simple

00:46:46.441 --> 00:46:48.660
that we can more or
less just have a few

00:46:48.661 --> 00:46:51.105
people read them and we'll
catch the major issues.

00:46:51.106 --> 00:46:53.150
The code will be so
short that's feasible.

00:46:53.151 --> 00:46:55.440
I want to try applying

00:46:55.441 --> 00:46:58.450
a proof carrying code approach
where we just say,

00:46:58.451 --> 00:47:00.730
if you want to run
your code on my system,

00:47:00.731 --> 00:47:02.425
you need to give me a proof

00:47:02.426 --> 00:47:03.735
of full functional correctness,

00:47:03.736 --> 00:47:05.265
or I'm not going to accept it.

00:47:05.266 --> 00:47:07.420
I think this is getting
to be feasible for

00:47:07.421 --> 00:47:09.730
both software and
digital hardware,

00:47:09.731 --> 00:47:12.620
and this should be doable
on an end-to-end way,

00:47:12.621 --> 00:47:13.980
where we're gonna take
all the proofs that we

00:47:13.981 --> 00:47:16.245
required to be provided
for all the components

00:47:16.246 --> 00:47:17.990
installed on the system and

00:47:17.991 --> 00:47:19.730
snap them all together
so that we get

00:47:19.731 --> 00:47:23.270
a very small TCB that contains
just our proof checker,

00:47:23.271 --> 00:47:24.520
as well as the semantics of

00:47:24.521 --> 00:47:28.450
sort of the top and bottom
level APIs of the system.

00:47:28.451 --> 00:47:30.630
Where the bottom is ideally,

00:47:30.631 --> 00:47:32.055
a hardware description language,

00:47:32.056 --> 00:47:33.795
and that the top is just the

00:47:33.796 --> 00:47:35.710
nice high-level
functional version

00:47:35.711 --> 00:47:37.340
of our applications and

00:47:37.341 --> 00:47:39.380
the system API view
that they see.

00:47:39.381 --> 00:47:40.685
If we do this right,

00:47:40.686 --> 00:47:42.260
each of those top
and bottom levels

00:47:42.261 --> 00:47:44.080
should be on the order
of 1,000 lines of

00:47:44.081 --> 00:47:48.175
code and very doable to audit
and can touch major issues.

00:47:48.176 --> 00:47:49.725
We should be able to,

00:47:49.726 --> 00:47:51.590
by using this proof approach,

00:47:51.591 --> 00:47:55.490
avoid all runtime
isolation mechanisms,

00:47:55.491 --> 00:47:59.215
and push all of our reasoning
about that into proofs.

00:47:59.216 --> 00:48:01.835
So, here's an example of

00:48:01.836 --> 00:48:04.320
a stack of pieces that could
head in that direction.

00:48:04.321 --> 00:48:05.710
So I want to start with

00:48:05.711 --> 00:48:07.800
the top-level view
of the system is,

00:48:07.801 --> 00:48:09.210
nice functional programs that

00:48:09.211 --> 00:48:10.880
we can view as the specification,

00:48:10.881 --> 00:48:13.360
and they would use
design patterns

00:48:13.361 --> 00:48:14.780
like object capabilities,

00:48:14.781 --> 00:48:17.275
so that just by
construction we achieve

00:48:17.276 --> 00:48:20.580
proper least privilege and we

00:48:20.581 --> 00:48:22.400
control the information flow

00:48:22.401 --> 00:48:23.980
graph within parts of a system,

00:48:23.981 --> 00:48:26.180
we achieve access
control just using

00:48:26.181 --> 00:48:29.130
pointers in a type safe language.

00:48:29.131 --> 00:48:32.430
Then we can rely

00:48:32.431 --> 00:48:34.760
on a compiler that's going
to produce C-like code

00:48:34.761 --> 00:48:36.760
from that point that does things

00:48:36.761 --> 00:48:39.790
like static analysis to
infer object lifetimes,

00:48:39.791 --> 00:48:41.820
to insert manual memory
management code so we

00:48:41.821 --> 00:48:44.850
won't need anything like
a garbage collector.

00:48:45.150 --> 00:48:49.200
Then our systems level of

00:48:49.201 --> 00:48:52.430
programming language wouldn't
need to have a type system,

00:48:52.431 --> 00:48:53.780
because if we're
already committing to

00:48:53.781 --> 00:48:55.650
proving everything
functionally correct,

00:48:55.651 --> 00:48:57.870
then a type system is
just an approximation

00:48:57.871 --> 00:48:59.720
of functional correctness.

00:48:59.721 --> 00:49:01.775
So we can leave
that out and expose

00:49:01.776 --> 00:49:04.155
a view of memory as
an array of bytes directly,

00:49:04.156 --> 00:49:06.150
but with structured
control-flow and functions

00:49:06.151 --> 00:49:08.815
and other good stuff
like that on top of it.

00:49:08.816 --> 00:49:11.020
The compiler for
that language will do things

00:49:11.021 --> 00:49:13.150
like worst-case
running time analysis.

00:49:13.151 --> 00:49:15.590
Tell us how long a function
might take to run.

00:49:15.591 --> 00:49:18.130
This might depend
on some annotations

00:49:18.131 --> 00:49:19.450
the programmer needs
to provide and

00:49:19.451 --> 00:49:20.980
maybe prove some things about.

00:49:20.981 --> 00:49:22.825
But then once you've done that,

00:49:22.826 --> 00:49:25.880
we can output machine code
which is prepared to run on

00:49:25.881 --> 00:49:28.950
machines in the
microcontroller class,

00:49:28.951 --> 00:49:30.250
that was one of the parts of

00:49:30.251 --> 00:49:31.795
the diagram the last talk showed,

00:49:31.796 --> 00:49:33.900
where we don't have
virtual memory

00:49:33.901 --> 00:49:36.100
or other address-space
protection.

00:49:36.101 --> 00:49:38.040
We don't have interrupts.

00:49:38.041 --> 00:49:39.730
We used to have a machine
that knows how to run

00:49:39.731 --> 00:49:42.250
machine code functions
when you ask it to.

00:49:42.251 --> 00:49:45.575
So then, what does
the processor do?

00:49:45.576 --> 00:49:48.260
It keeps receiving IO events

00:49:48.261 --> 00:49:49.715
from the world and for each one,

00:49:49.716 --> 00:49:51.630
it knows which function to call

00:49:51.631 --> 00:49:55.145
in the pilot machine code
that we've produced,

00:49:55.146 --> 00:49:56.630
and that function
just runs to the end

00:49:56.631 --> 00:49:58.450
and decides what to
do next including

00:49:58.451 --> 00:50:02.265
signaling any response output
events that should happen.

00:50:02.266 --> 00:50:03.650
It's safe to avoid using

00:50:03.651 --> 00:50:05.310
interrupts or anything
like that here,

00:50:05.311 --> 00:50:06.930
because we did
worst case execution

00:50:06.931 --> 00:50:08.950
time analysis and
all of the code,

00:50:08.951 --> 00:50:11.210
we don't depend on
virtual memory to isolate

00:50:11.211 --> 00:50:13.320
the different parts of
the system because that was all

00:50:13.321 --> 00:50:15.230
present in the original
functional code

00:50:15.231 --> 00:50:18.120
through object capability system
and other such patterns

00:50:18.121 --> 00:50:20.650
and preserved through
semantic guarantees

00:50:20.651 --> 00:50:23.570
about all the levels bringing
us down to this point.

00:50:23.571 --> 00:50:26.440
One thing that's kind
of hard about good,

00:50:26.441 --> 00:50:29.695
worst case execution time
analysis of realistic code is,

00:50:29.696 --> 00:50:31.530
predicting the cost of

00:50:31.531 --> 00:50:34.270
memory operations given
complex cache systems.

00:50:34.271 --> 00:50:36.350
So one other idea that
we're thinking about

00:50:36.351 --> 00:50:39.225
is taking the high-level view
we have of code,

00:50:39.226 --> 00:50:43.190
to preserve down through
this whole stack proved

00:50:43.191 --> 00:50:45.790
accurate characterizations
of which objects in

00:50:45.791 --> 00:50:48.680
memory each function
is going to need,

00:50:48.681 --> 00:50:51.100
so that the the memory system

00:50:51.101 --> 00:50:52.860
in the cache
coherence protocol can

00:50:52.861 --> 00:50:54.730
move into place in the cache

00:50:54.731 --> 00:50:57.300
whichever processor gets
picked to run a function,

00:50:57.301 --> 00:50:59.300
guaranteed all the
object is going

00:50:59.301 --> 00:51:01.185
to need so that when we
actually run the code,

00:51:01.186 --> 00:51:03.830
we know exactly how long
it's going to take or

00:51:03.831 --> 00:51:04.970
we can at least get a much more

00:51:04.971 --> 00:51:07.140
accurate bound than before.

00:51:07.141 --> 00:51:10.075
So that's the idea there.

00:51:10.076 --> 00:51:13.960
To summarize, I think it's
worth exploring how some of

00:51:13.961 --> 00:51:16.020
the challenges that we're
used to seeing as fundamental

00:51:16.021 --> 00:51:18.820
in the design of
systems software,

00:51:18.821 --> 00:51:21.095
can really be re-arranged by

00:51:21.096 --> 00:51:23.180
committing to functional
correctness proofs

00:51:23.181 --> 00:51:24.900
for all parts of the system.

00:51:24.901 --> 00:51:27.050
This technology has
really advanced a lot.

00:51:27.051 --> 00:51:29.210
I think in the next
10 years or so,

00:51:29.211 --> 00:51:31.435
this will be the mainstream,

00:51:31.436 --> 00:51:33.340
and I think it's
interesting to consider

00:51:33.341 --> 00:51:35.635
what domains there
are out there where,

00:51:35.636 --> 00:51:36.895
if we wanted to re-implement

00:51:36.896 --> 00:51:39.580
all the complete software
stack from scratch,

00:51:39.581 --> 00:51:42.600
this would be pretty feasible,

00:51:42.601 --> 00:51:46.779
and I think there are
more cases of that than

00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:48.700
we'd think from
the usual conversations

00:51:48.701 --> 00:51:51.870
about this sort of stuff. Thanks.

00:51:58.280 --> 00:52:07.770
Questions.

00:52:07.771 --> 00:52:08.880
&gt;&gt; It's very impressive.

00:52:08.881 --> 00:52:11.025
Could you say a bit about

00:52:11.026 --> 00:52:14.400
how you would have
caught the problems

00:52:14.401 --> 00:52:17.580
of interference between
speculative execution

00:52:17.581 --> 00:52:20.195
and caching in this picture?

00:52:20.196 --> 00:52:22.600
And more generally,

00:52:22.601 --> 00:52:25.135
our processor abstractions have

00:52:25.136 --> 00:52:27.280
the property that there are a lot

00:52:27.281 --> 00:52:29.100
of details we don't
know about and in

00:52:29.101 --> 00:52:31.780
a sense abstractions
are bad for security

00:52:31.781 --> 00:52:33.680
because they hide from

00:52:33.681 --> 00:52:36.900
the implementer properties that

00:52:36.901 --> 00:52:39.045
the attacker takes advantage of.

00:52:39.046 --> 00:52:40.960
So how does that fit into

00:52:40.961 --> 00:52:44.920
this picture that's strongly
based on abstractions?

00:52:44.921 --> 00:52:47.225
&gt;&gt; So let me start with
the second question or

00:52:47.226 --> 00:52:49.735
maybe try to clarify
the intent of it.

00:52:49.736 --> 00:52:51.870
If you're talking about
abstraction through

00:52:51.871 --> 00:52:54.055
closed hardware designs from

00:52:54.056 --> 00:52:55.935
manufacturers out there today,

00:52:55.936 --> 00:52:57.905
then that doesn't fit so
well into our picture.

00:52:57.906 --> 00:52:59.080
But if you think
about open-source

00:52:59.081 --> 00:53:00.520
hardware designs like
are coming up in

00:53:00.521 --> 00:53:03.510
the context of
the risk 5 instruction set,

00:53:03.511 --> 00:53:06.520
then that fits completely
well into this picture.

00:53:06.521 --> 00:53:08.815
And we're doing some work
in that area ourselves,

00:53:08.816 --> 00:53:12.550
where you can assign
a semantics to

00:53:12.551 --> 00:53:14.120
the hardware description
language that

00:53:14.121 --> 00:53:17.380
includes timing of
all interactions

00:53:17.381 --> 00:53:18.675
with the outside world,

00:53:18.676 --> 00:53:21.770
and you can formulate what
it means for timing to be

00:53:21.771 --> 00:53:25.085
independent of secret inputs
coming in on certain wires.

00:53:25.086 --> 00:53:28.020
And then if you managed
to prove that theorem,

00:53:28.021 --> 00:53:30.780
then you really have avoided
spectrum meltdown attacks.

00:53:30.781 --> 00:53:32.080
But there is a challenge
there that I

00:53:32.081 --> 00:53:33.560
think the architecture
community doesn't

00:53:33.561 --> 00:53:35.170
quite know yet what
the actual changes

00:53:35.171 --> 00:53:36.865
to the workings of
processors are,

00:53:36.866 --> 00:53:39.315
to avoid those issues
while keeping performance.

00:53:39.316 --> 00:53:42.100
So we'll just try
to be ready with

00:53:42.101 --> 00:53:43.240
general proof tools for

00:53:43.241 --> 00:53:46.180
whenever those
mechanisms are known.

00:53:53.690 --> 00:53:55.650
&gt;&gt; So I've got a question.

00:53:55.651 --> 00:53:55.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:53:55.951 --> 00:54:00.430
&gt;&gt; So related to timing,

00:54:01.190 --> 00:54:03.500
when people write
these functional

00:54:03.501 --> 00:54:05.185
correctness specifications

00:54:05.186 --> 00:54:07.975
how much do you expect
people to say about timing?

00:54:07.976 --> 00:54:09.580
Like if they say I'm going

00:54:09.581 --> 00:54:12.120
to send five messages
to somebody,

00:54:12.121 --> 00:54:13.910
do they have to say exactly

00:54:13.911 --> 00:54:15.510
when they're going to
send those messages so

00:54:15.511 --> 00:54:17.590
they don't leak any bits

00:54:17.591 --> 00:54:19.565
through the timing
of the messages?

00:54:19.566 --> 00:54:24.080
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:54:24.081 --> 00:54:25.360
One approach is to require

00:54:25.361 --> 00:54:26.990
high level functional
specifications

00:54:26.991 --> 00:54:28.840
that for each operation
you can run,

00:54:28.841 --> 00:54:30.330
the functional program needs to

00:54:30.331 --> 00:54:32.120
compute the running time

00:54:32.121 --> 00:54:34.235
that it expects to have
in the real world.

00:54:34.236 --> 00:54:36.380
That can be an opaque function,

00:54:36.381 --> 00:54:37.570
you don't know what
it is but you know

00:54:37.571 --> 00:54:40.250
it's well-defined
and deterministic.

00:54:40.690 --> 00:54:44.690
It's hard to see how to avoid
heading in that direction,

00:54:44.691 --> 00:54:46.950
to really avoid
all the information leaks

00:54:46.951 --> 00:54:49.730
but this is pretty
speculative right now.

00:54:56.320 --> 00:54:58.660
&gt;&gt; Thanks. One question is

00:54:58.661 --> 00:55:02.605
about observing side channels
other than timing.

00:55:02.606 --> 00:55:05.935
So for example, power side
channels or radio emissions.

00:55:05.936 --> 00:55:09.690
What aspects of your work
do you think translate

00:55:09.691 --> 00:55:11.420
well from timing analysis

00:55:11.421 --> 00:55:13.735
to other analysis and
what aspects don't?

00:55:13.736 --> 00:55:16.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah so those other
side channels I'm

00:55:16.081 --> 00:55:18.925
really not very
well-informed about but if

00:55:18.926 --> 00:55:20.510
someone who was
could come up with

00:55:20.511 --> 00:55:24.030
some security condition that

00:55:24.031 --> 00:55:26.560
applies to RTL designs then we

00:55:26.561 --> 00:55:29.550
can probably incorporate
that in this framework.

00:55:29.551 --> 00:55:31.770
I'm not informed enough to know

00:55:31.771 --> 00:55:34.720
what that kind of
condition might look like.

00:55:34.721 --> 00:55:37.300
&gt;&gt; So as you were
presenting the crypto-work,

00:55:37.301 --> 00:55:39.780
the voice in the back of
my head was saying oh yes but

00:55:39.781 --> 00:55:40.960
crypto-code has a nice clean

00:55:40.961 --> 00:55:42.650
functional spec and it's
all self-contained.

00:55:42.651 --> 00:55:43.370
It doesn't have to deal with

00:55:43.371 --> 00:55:44.940
memory models and e-drops
and all these things.

00:55:44.941 --> 00:55:46.990
And then we get to the end of
your talk and it seems like

00:55:46.991 --> 00:55:50.405
your answer to that problem
is, we'll make it go away.

00:55:50.406 --> 00:55:54.030
That's a great clean slate
design but do you have

00:55:54.031 --> 00:55:55.180
any thoughts about how we get

00:55:55.181 --> 00:55:57.085
from here to there incrementally?

00:55:57.086 --> 00:56:00.745
Because that sounds like
a hard change to make.

00:56:00.746 --> 00:56:02.130
&gt;&gt; We-

00:56:02.131 --> 00:56:03.820
&gt;&gt; The crypto stuff
is easy to deploy

00:56:03.821 --> 00:56:07.260
because it plugs into
the existing system and it,

00:56:07.261 --> 00:56:08.975
observably makes it better.

00:56:08.976 --> 00:56:10.420
But I don't see how you could

00:56:10.421 --> 00:56:12.100
incrementally deploy
something like this.

00:56:12.101 --> 00:56:13.125
Have you thought about that?

00:56:13.126 --> 00:56:14.630
&gt;&gt; So I think I'm not suggesting

00:56:14.631 --> 00:56:16.310
incremental deployment
in that sense but

00:56:16.311 --> 00:56:17.830
just building some cool boxes of

00:56:17.831 --> 00:56:19.590
the kind we heard about
in the last talk.

00:56:19.591 --> 00:56:20.950
Using this framework which

00:56:20.951 --> 00:56:22.160
then talked to
the rest of the world

00:56:22.161 --> 00:56:25.650
through standard
protocols and interfaces.

00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:31.715
&gt;&gt; Any other questions?
Just go ahead.

00:56:31.716 --> 00:56:39.365
Thanks Chris.

00:56:39.366 --> 00:56:41.950
So this is a talk
about project Everest

00:56:41.951 --> 00:56:44.380
which is a joint effort

00:56:44.381 --> 00:56:47.500
among several
Microsoft labs as well

00:56:47.501 --> 00:56:51.715
as INRIA and Carnegie Mellon.

00:56:51.716 --> 00:56:54.490
It's a project that
we've had going

00:56:54.491 --> 00:56:56.690
for a couple of years
now that's aiming

00:56:56.691 --> 00:56:58.270
to build and deploy

00:56:58.271 --> 00:57:02.400
verified components in
the HTTPS ecosystem.

00:57:02.401 --> 00:57:06.885
So I think you know
what HTTPS is,

00:57:06.886 --> 00:57:10.545
even if you don't use it
pretty sure everyday.

00:57:10.546 --> 00:57:12.100
Essentially when you connect on

00:57:12.101 --> 00:57:13.690
the web to any secure website,

00:57:13.691 --> 00:57:17.420
whether it's a bank
or your email,

00:57:17.421 --> 00:57:22.045
you negotiated a secure channel
using a protocol,

00:57:22.046 --> 00:57:31.040
a cryptographic protocol
that underlies HTTPS.

00:57:31.041 --> 00:57:36.960
So on the surface the most
common usages through

00:57:36.961 --> 00:57:40.095
this web protocol but

00:57:40.096 --> 00:57:44.245
within HTTPS is is actually
quite a complex ecosystem.

00:57:44.246 --> 00:57:47.540
At the heart of it is a protocol

00:57:47.541 --> 00:57:50.105
called TLS, transport
layer security,

00:57:50.106 --> 00:57:53.269
beneath which lie
several crypto-algorithms

00:57:53.270 --> 00:57:55.380
and alongside it are

00:57:55.381 --> 00:57:57.710
things like the Public
Key Infrastructure,

00:57:57.711 --> 00:58:02.900
ultimately talking to
the network through a TCP.

00:58:02.901 --> 00:58:05.355
Above it are clients and servers

00:58:05.356 --> 00:58:08.150
that incorporate
this suite of protocols.

00:58:08.151 --> 00:58:12.675
The trouble with HTTPS is that

00:58:12.676 --> 00:58:15.200
although you should still use
it and it's significantly

00:58:15.201 --> 00:58:18.740
better than not using
any protection at all,

00:58:19.910 --> 00:58:22.640
the protocols in the space
and the algorithms

00:58:22.641 --> 00:58:24.560
in this space had
been hit by attacks

00:58:24.561 --> 00:58:29.490
and corresponding fixes
for 20 years or more.

00:58:29.491 --> 00:58:33.390
And maybe the one that's got

00:58:33.391 --> 00:58:35.790
the most attention is
this Heartbleed Bug

00:58:35.791 --> 00:58:38.190
which in a way is
a very boring bug.

00:58:38.191 --> 00:58:43.335
Heartbleed was essentially
exploiting a buffer overrun,

00:58:43.336 --> 00:58:47.265
a memory-safety vulnerability to

00:58:47.266 --> 00:58:49.590
compromise
implementations of TLS.

00:58:49.591 --> 00:58:51.240
But there have been
other kinds of bugs too

00:58:51.241 --> 00:58:53.675
that are independent
of implementations.

00:58:53.676 --> 00:58:55.795
There have been design flaws in

00:58:55.796 --> 00:59:02.855
the protocols and all the
mainstream implementations

00:59:02.856 --> 00:59:05.920
have been subject to these flaws.

00:59:05.921 --> 00:59:10.300
OpenSSL is the most widely
used implementation

00:59:10.301 --> 00:59:13.570
but Microsoft's SChannel
is also very widely used.

00:59:13.571 --> 00:59:15.400
There's NSS from Mozilla.

00:59:15.401 --> 00:59:17.460
There other boring SSL things

00:59:17.461 --> 00:59:18.770
that have forked from OpenSSL.

00:59:18.771 --> 00:59:20.660
All of them have

00:59:20.661 --> 00:59:24.815
been attacked and get
repeatedly patched.

00:59:24.816 --> 00:59:29.220
So in 2016 we started a project

00:59:29.221 --> 00:59:31.410
that had a five-year scope

00:59:31.411 --> 00:59:33.455
so we're about
halfway through it,

00:59:33.456 --> 00:59:35.335
and we had a few goals.

00:59:35.336 --> 00:59:37.975
Our first goal was to build

00:59:37.976 --> 00:59:45.800
a verified implementation
of the components in HTTPS.

00:59:45.801 --> 00:59:48.600
Our goal is to actually
build these components in

00:59:48.601 --> 00:59:51.205
a form where they are low level

00:59:51.206 --> 00:59:54.210
and are efficient enough to be

00:59:54.211 --> 00:59:59.020
deployed as is inside
the existing software ecosystem.

00:59:59.060 --> 01:00:01.875
In doing so we are

01:00:01.876 --> 01:00:05.340
building and improving
verification tools

01:00:05.341 --> 01:00:07.015
that have been developed in MSR.

01:00:07.016 --> 01:00:09.035
Fstar is a programming
language that

01:00:09.036 --> 01:00:11.195
is a proof assistant in
the spirit of something like

01:00:11.196 --> 01:00:13.770
cURL that Adam mentioned

01:00:13.771 --> 01:00:17.410
but using an automated
theorem prover called,

01:00:17.411 --> 01:00:20.690
Z3, that's also been
developed at Microsoft.

01:00:20.691 --> 01:00:26.330
So, in pursuing project

01:00:26.331 --> 01:00:28.860
ever us in building these
verified components we also aim

01:00:28.861 --> 01:00:33.565
to produce more trustworthy
usable verification tools

01:00:33.566 --> 01:00:36.140
and to grow expertise in

01:00:36.141 --> 01:00:37.330
building this kind of verified

01:00:37.331 --> 01:00:39.415
software at this kind of scale.

01:00:39.416 --> 01:00:43.470
Finally, we strongly
believe that this kind of

01:00:43.471 --> 01:00:45.130
security work has to
be done in the open

01:00:45.131 --> 01:00:46.990
so everything that we
do is is open-source.

01:00:46.991 --> 01:00:51.035
Including the tools,
Fstar is open-source

01:00:51.036 --> 01:00:57.445
and all the verified
software that we build.

01:00:57.446 --> 01:00:59.070
Were a large team of people who

01:00:59.071 --> 01:01:02.970
who work on this at
several MSR lab so,

01:01:02.971 --> 01:01:05.760
Redmond, Cambridge and India,

01:01:05.761 --> 01:01:07.460
several of whom are in the room.

01:01:07.461 --> 01:01:09.305
There's Chris and Tahena and

01:01:09.306 --> 01:01:11.650
Jonathon at least that
I see who work on

01:01:11.651 --> 01:01:13.985
Everest so feel free to

01:01:13.986 --> 01:01:17.950
come find us and ask us
questions about this.

01:01:18.860 --> 01:01:24.360
Aside from the MSR labs we
have collaborators at Inria in

01:01:24.361 --> 01:01:27.030
Paris who we worked with there's

01:01:27.031 --> 01:01:29.220
an MSR Inria joint
center at Paris

01:01:29.221 --> 01:01:32.495
and we work with them
through this arrangement.

01:01:32.496 --> 01:01:35.455
We have collaborators at CMU

01:01:35.456 --> 01:01:37.765
and at the University
of Edinburgh,

01:01:37.766 --> 01:01:40.800
and a long list of interns,

01:01:40.801 --> 01:01:42.685
visitors and so on.

01:01:42.686 --> 01:01:44.330
We're very open to collaboration

01:01:44.331 --> 01:01:46.230
so if you're interested
in this kind of stuff

01:01:46.231 --> 01:01:50.610
and would like to work
with us get in touch.

01:01:51.610 --> 01:01:57.600
So here's where we are
at two and a half years,

01:01:58.520 --> 01:02:01.105
we have verified implementations

01:02:01.106 --> 01:02:04.340
of most of the the
key cipher suites

01:02:04.341 --> 01:02:09.780
that are used in TLS.

01:02:09.781 --> 01:02:11.780
For the moment, not look so

01:02:11.781 --> 01:02:13.715
much at the public-key
infrastructure,

01:02:13.716 --> 01:02:16.430
but have instead been looking
at a protocol called QUIC,

01:02:16.431 --> 01:02:19.375
which is a new development
in this ecosystem.

01:02:19.376 --> 01:02:22.390
So, QUIC is a new
networking protocol

01:02:22.391 --> 01:02:27.415
that is undergoing a standardization
process at the IETF,

01:02:27.416 --> 01:02:29.410
driven initially by Google,

01:02:29.411 --> 01:02:33.235
and it's trying to
do TLS over UDP

01:02:33.236 --> 01:02:38.030
in a new clean slate redesign.

01:02:38.031 --> 01:02:44.320
So we have deployments
of our verified code,

01:02:44.321 --> 01:02:48.450
in tests deployments
in three clients,

01:02:48.451 --> 01:02:52.230
the Edge browser,
the cURL library that lets you

01:02:52.231 --> 01:02:56.130
do command line code is
actually used in many,

01:02:56.131 --> 01:02:59.470
many tools, but one
of it's usage is

01:02:59.471 --> 01:03:03.115
just fetching pages from
a festering web content.

01:03:03.116 --> 01:03:05.150
Ngnix is a Open Source Web Server

01:03:05.151 --> 01:03:07.235
and we have a test
deployment in there,

01:03:07.236 --> 01:03:09.680
but aside from
these test deployments,

01:03:09.681 --> 01:03:13.835
we have two main production
scale deployments.

01:03:13.836 --> 01:03:17.105
So, Windows is developing

01:03:17.106 --> 01:03:20.180
an implementation of
QUIC called WinQUIC,

01:03:20.181 --> 01:03:25.350
and WinQUIC uses TLS and
Everest is TLS runs in

01:03:25.351 --> 01:03:27.400
WinQUIC and is now part

01:03:27.401 --> 01:03:32.050
of the Windows
development builds.

01:03:32.190 --> 01:03:35.035
We also, being Open Source,

01:03:35.036 --> 01:03:37.610
our code runs also
outside Microsoft,

01:03:37.611 --> 01:03:43.160
so Mozilla's NSS library
which is used in Firefox and

01:03:43.161 --> 01:03:46.180
another Mozilla tools runs

01:03:46.181 --> 01:03:50.095
Everest Crypto specifically
the Crypto-algorithms,

01:03:50.096 --> 01:03:53.110
not yet the protocol stack.

01:03:55.500 --> 01:03:57.950
So, when talking about

01:03:57.951 --> 01:04:00.460
verification there's
always a question of like,

01:04:00.461 --> 01:04:02.460
well, what do you
actually verify?

01:04:02.461 --> 01:04:04.770
As a baseline for
all this kind of

01:04:04.771 --> 01:04:06.980
work, we verify safety.

01:04:06.981 --> 01:04:09.500
So without memory safety you

01:04:09.501 --> 01:04:12.595
can't really get very far in
proving anything further.

01:04:12.596 --> 01:04:14.110
That's the baseline level of

01:04:14.111 --> 01:04:15.660
abstraction that
you need to prove,

01:04:15.661 --> 01:04:17.950
so we prove safety
for all our code.

01:04:17.951 --> 01:04:22.170
Then depending on the component
that we're verifying,

01:04:22.171 --> 01:04:24.025
we pick properties that

01:04:24.026 --> 01:04:26.395
we think are the most suitable
for those components.

01:04:26.396 --> 01:04:29.165
So usually, this means
at least functional correctness,

01:04:29.166 --> 01:04:31.665
but for certain components,

01:04:31.666 --> 01:04:36.415
we will also prove things
like cryptographic security,

01:04:36.416 --> 01:04:39.020
meaning that we bound

01:04:39.021 --> 01:04:40.540
the probability that
an attacker can

01:04:40.541 --> 01:04:43.760
break a certain
crypto-construction.

01:04:47.040 --> 01:04:49.640
Despite all our proofs,

01:04:49.641 --> 01:04:52.940
you may ask, will Everest
be perfectly secure?

01:04:52.941 --> 01:04:54.980
The answer to this is no.

01:04:54.981 --> 01:04:57.240
All this verification
work is based

01:04:57.241 --> 01:04:59.295
on a set of assumptions,

01:04:59.296 --> 01:05:01.835
both with respect to the
specifications that you write.

01:05:01.836 --> 01:05:04.760
So, if you're skeptical

01:05:04.761 --> 01:05:05.960
about Everest or any

01:05:05.961 --> 01:05:07.470
verification process
for that matter,

01:05:07.471 --> 01:05:09.470
you need to study
the specifications

01:05:09.471 --> 01:05:10.760
that were actually
proven by the code and

01:05:10.761 --> 01:05:12.640
convince yourself that
the specs actually

01:05:12.641 --> 01:05:16.040
mean what you think they mean.

01:05:16.041 --> 01:05:18.975
Specs are written with
respect to a model,

01:05:18.976 --> 01:05:21.320
and our models try to be detailed

01:05:21.321 --> 01:05:23.905
and cover all classes
of known attacks,

01:05:23.906 --> 01:05:25.510
but there may be

01:05:25.511 --> 01:05:27.765
new classes of attacks
that we're not aware of

01:05:27.766 --> 01:05:35.000
that may become more
or less inadequate.

01:05:35.001 --> 01:05:38.130
Also a verification
tool chain places

01:05:38.131 --> 01:05:41.260
trust in a number of
components including F-star,

01:05:41.261 --> 01:05:46.670
the Z3 term proven that we
use on C compilers and so on.

01:05:46.671 --> 01:05:49.565
Those are part of
the trusted computing base.

01:05:49.566 --> 01:05:51.725
Despite these limitations,

01:05:51.726 --> 01:05:53.990
the general methodology of using

01:05:53.991 --> 01:05:57.925
computer-aided verification
has some clear advantages;

01:05:57.926 --> 01:05:59.780
verification is automated.

01:05:59.781 --> 01:06:01.130
It may take several hours,

01:06:01.131 --> 01:06:02.345
but you make a change,

01:06:02.346 --> 01:06:03.840
and you want to
know if your change

01:06:03.841 --> 01:06:06.565
was breaking a system invariant.

01:06:06.566 --> 01:06:09.270
Well, the verification system
is going to tell

01:06:09.271 --> 01:06:12.550
you as part of a continuous
integration system.

01:06:12.551 --> 01:06:15.460
It's the way in which you can get

01:06:15.461 --> 01:06:16.765
approved to work at this scale is

01:06:16.766 --> 01:06:18.470
by breaking it up
into components,

01:06:18.471 --> 01:06:23.240
and so verification is
modular and compositional,

01:06:23.241 --> 01:06:26.660
meaning that you can
take as a component say,

01:06:26.661 --> 01:06:30.090
a particular crypto
construction that we use

01:06:30.091 --> 01:06:32.370
inside TLS and also use it in

01:06:32.371 --> 01:06:36.060
some other component
which is for instance,

01:06:36.061 --> 01:06:39.305
our usage of TLS and QUIC
is a great example of this.

01:06:39.306 --> 01:06:41.980
TLS was intended for
use initially over TCP,

01:06:41.981 --> 01:06:45.470
but the same crypto protocol

01:06:45.471 --> 01:06:49.090
can also be used in
a different setting like QUIC.

01:06:52.770 --> 01:06:55.700
So, the programming and
verification tools that we

01:06:55.701 --> 01:06:58.200
use at the source level

01:06:58.201 --> 01:07:00.835
is a programming
language called F-star.

01:07:00.836 --> 01:07:03.970
F-star makes use of Z3 to

01:07:03.971 --> 01:07:06.460
prove verification
conditions that

01:07:06.461 --> 01:07:08.740
arise as you try to
write your program.

01:07:08.741 --> 01:07:12.180
So, after you write your
F-star program and verify it,

01:07:12.181 --> 01:07:14.865
you can compile it
either to OCaml

01:07:14.866 --> 01:07:18.620
or in a tool that we've
developed as part of Everest.

01:07:18.621 --> 01:07:20.965
You can extract
your F-star code to C,

01:07:20.966 --> 01:07:22.090
and then compile it using

01:07:22.091 --> 01:07:24.400
a C compiler and link it
with the rest of the system.

01:07:24.401 --> 01:07:28.120
So, let me do
a small demo of this.

01:07:28.450 --> 01:07:31.650
Is that big enough in the back?

01:07:31.660 --> 01:07:42.775
Yes? Okay. So, this is
F-star running in max,

01:07:42.776 --> 01:07:45.725
and an F-star program,

01:07:45.726 --> 01:07:47.470
it's syntax superficially
looks a bit

01:07:47.471 --> 01:07:51.070
like functional languages
like OCaml or F-sharp,

01:07:51.071 --> 01:07:55.835
but within this framework
you can encode other DSLs.

01:07:55.836 --> 01:07:58.130
So, I'm showing you
here a DSL that we have

01:07:58.131 --> 01:08:01.675
an F-star for writing
C like programs.

01:08:01.676 --> 01:08:05.430
This demo is a very simple
example that's showing

01:08:05.431 --> 01:08:09.145
how you may copy the contents
of one array to another.

01:08:09.146 --> 01:08:13.580
Copy from the Source
Array to the dest array.

01:08:13.650 --> 01:08:16.280
The way in which you do
this copy is really simple,

01:08:16.281 --> 01:08:21.015
it's a recursive function
that assigns the current byte

01:08:21.016 --> 01:08:23.070
of source to dest and then

01:08:23.071 --> 01:08:26.645
recurses on increasing
the counter by one.

01:08:26.646 --> 01:08:29.695
Now, when you try to write
such a program you're forced

01:08:29.696 --> 01:08:32.445
in F-star to prove various
basic properties about it.

01:08:32.446 --> 01:08:34.630
So, you see these
comments that I have

01:08:34.631 --> 01:08:37.695
here that say that at
various points in this code,

01:08:37.696 --> 01:08:39.315
F-star will force you to prove

01:08:39.316 --> 01:08:40.815
temporal safety for instance.

01:08:40.816 --> 01:08:42.080
Meaning that you cannot access

01:08:42.081 --> 01:08:44.410
a memory location unless

01:08:44.411 --> 01:08:46.875
you prove that the memory
location is currently live.

01:08:46.876 --> 01:08:51.460
Or when you do
an integer operation,

01:08:51.461 --> 01:08:53.375
you need to prove that
there's no integer overflow.

01:08:53.376 --> 01:08:55.550
So having written this code,

01:08:55.551 --> 01:08:57.070
you can ask F-star to check it,

01:08:57.071 --> 01:09:00.115
and it'll say, in
this case says yes.

01:09:00.116 --> 01:09:02.235
But if I forget
a precondition, for instance,

01:09:02.236 --> 01:09:05.250
I try to use the source array,

01:09:05.251 --> 01:09:07.800
without knowing that
the source array is live,

01:09:07.801 --> 01:09:09.845
and I asked if so to verify it,

01:09:09.846 --> 01:09:12.060
it'll complain
pointing at a location

01:09:12.061 --> 01:09:15.100
here that says, "Well,

01:09:15.101 --> 01:09:17.480
at that index this says live",

01:09:17.481 --> 01:09:19.760
you couldn't prove
that the buffer

01:09:19.761 --> 01:09:22.890
that you were accessing
was live at that point.

01:09:22.930 --> 01:09:27.230
So, this first attempt
here is just proving

01:09:27.231 --> 01:09:31.335
that copy is safe to run.

01:09:31.336 --> 01:09:32.500
It doesn't prove anything about

01:09:32.501 --> 01:09:33.950
what copy actually accomplishes.

01:09:33.951 --> 01:09:35.920
So, this bit of the spec

01:09:35.921 --> 01:09:38.950
here is an insurer's
clause which says,

01:09:38.951 --> 01:09:40.500
at the end of running this code,

01:09:40.501 --> 01:09:42.110
there's something that
you can assume is

01:09:42.111 --> 01:09:44.035
true about the state
of the program,

01:09:44.036 --> 01:09:46.195
and in this case
the ensures process is true.

01:09:46.196 --> 01:09:48.450
So, you don't get
anything if you don't

01:09:48.451 --> 01:09:49.670
get any functional
correctness property

01:09:49.671 --> 01:09:51.230
out of this version of copy.

01:09:51.231 --> 01:09:53.775
But here's another
version of copy

01:09:53.776 --> 01:09:57.495
where it's the same code
as I showed you before,

01:09:57.496 --> 01:09:58.720
but it's now augmented with

01:09:58.721 --> 01:10:01.410
the spec that tells
you that after you run

01:10:01.411 --> 01:10:05.680
copy in the final memory H1

01:10:05.681 --> 01:10:07.620
that results after
running this program,

01:10:07.621 --> 01:10:10.390
the source and the destination
buffers are equal,

01:10:10.391 --> 01:10:14.395
which is what you might
expect from doing copy.

01:10:14.396 --> 01:10:20.560
So, F-star like such agrees
with the specification.

01:10:20.561 --> 01:10:24.180
So having verified your program,

01:10:24.181 --> 01:10:30.395
you can compile your program
to see and here,

01:10:30.396 --> 01:10:32.125
I did this just a
little while ago,

01:10:32.126 --> 01:10:35.300
so this is an extraction of
the previous program to see,

01:10:35.301 --> 01:10:37.550
and notice that when
you extract a C

01:10:37.551 --> 01:10:39.210
you just get C-code
that you would

01:10:39.211 --> 01:10:41.830
expect with
all the specifications

01:10:42.320 --> 01:10:46.545
erased and with no remaining
runtime checks or anything.

01:10:46.546 --> 01:10:47.970
All the verification is static.

01:10:47.971 --> 01:10:50.090
So, the stellar
verification generally

01:10:50.091 --> 01:10:53.820
imposes no additional runtime
performance overhead.

01:10:54.330 --> 01:10:59.660
Alright. So, that's
a sub language

01:10:59.661 --> 01:11:01.620
that I showed you in F-star for

01:11:01.621 --> 01:11:04.335
programming at the C-level
of abstraction,

01:11:04.336 --> 01:11:06.015
but to get performance code

01:11:06.016 --> 01:11:07.985
especially in some of
these crypto applications,

01:11:07.986 --> 01:11:11.080
you need to go beyond C, and so,

01:11:11.081 --> 01:11:16.590
we have another DSL called
Vale which is a DSL

01:11:16.591 --> 01:11:19.470
embedded in F-star
for programming at

01:11:19.471 --> 01:11:21.310
the assembly level of

01:11:21.311 --> 01:11:23.720
building verified
assembly programs.

01:11:24.100 --> 01:11:27.500
So, with these tools

01:11:27.501 --> 01:11:32.310
we aim to build
TLS protocols like it,

01:11:32.311 --> 01:11:35.130
and for TLS the domain security
goal that you're after

01:11:35.131 --> 01:11:38.495
is to show that TLS
establishes a secure channel.

01:11:38.496 --> 01:11:40.140
Meaning that you get
a channel between

01:11:40.141 --> 01:11:43.305
the end points that is authentic,

01:11:43.306 --> 01:11:45.265
meaning that an adversary
cannot inject

01:11:45.266 --> 01:11:48.245
false data into the stream,
and is confidential.

01:11:48.246 --> 01:11:50.630
Meaning that an anniversary
cannot distinguish

01:11:50.631 --> 01:11:52.460
between the communication of

01:11:52.461 --> 01:11:55.280
random bytes and
a secret on the stream.

01:11:57.080 --> 01:11:59.910
The TLS protocol has
through the years

01:11:59.911 --> 01:12:04.020
been designed has been
vulnerable in many ways,

01:12:04.021 --> 01:12:08.560
but TLS 1.3 is the latest
version of the protocol

01:12:08.561 --> 01:12:13.745
that is now a ITF
proposed standard.

01:12:13.746 --> 01:12:17.720
In the process of us
verifying TLS 1.3,

01:12:17.721 --> 01:12:22.620
we discovered many
potential protocol flaws

01:12:22.621 --> 01:12:25.620
in earlier versions of
the design and proposed.

01:12:25.621 --> 01:12:31.100
I think we have
six proposals that have been

01:12:31.101 --> 01:12:33.630
incorporated in the proposed
standard that arose as

01:12:33.631 --> 01:12:37.210
a result of us doing
our verification work.

01:12:37.630 --> 01:12:40.510
The ITF has generally been quite

01:12:40.511 --> 01:12:42.670
receptive to this at least in

01:12:42.671 --> 01:12:44.720
the TLS Committee has
been receptive to

01:12:44.721 --> 01:12:47.515
this kind of formal work as well.

01:12:47.516 --> 01:12:51.280
The standard for instance
cites both hours, as well as,

01:12:51.281 --> 01:12:53.050
several other formal models

01:12:53.051 --> 01:12:59.440
of TLS as a validation of
the design of the protocol.

01:13:00.440 --> 01:13:02.860
So, the way in which we model

01:13:02.861 --> 01:13:06.660
cryptographic security and prove

01:13:06.661 --> 01:13:10.390
it is in the following manner.

01:13:10.391 --> 01:13:12.970
So, you have a concrete
implementation of TLS

01:13:12.971 --> 01:13:16.000
that's using a crypto provider,

01:13:16.001 --> 01:13:16.830
a crypto library that's

01:13:16.831 --> 01:13:18.630
implementing
the actual algorithms,

01:13:18.631 --> 01:13:22.375
and you have
an application program

01:13:22.376 --> 01:13:24.040
which may also represent

01:13:24.041 --> 01:13:26.580
the adversary
interacting with TLS.

01:13:27.450 --> 01:13:33.250
What we do is a general proof
methodology is to show that

01:13:33.251 --> 01:13:36.310
this concrete version of TLS is

01:13:36.311 --> 01:13:39.690
indistinguishable from
an ideal version of TLS,

01:13:39.691 --> 01:13:41.230
about which we can prove that it

01:13:41.231 --> 01:13:43.595
actually implemented
secure channel.

01:13:43.596 --> 01:13:46.885
The way we do that is by
verifying the crypto library,

01:13:46.886 --> 01:13:51.690
and then making use of a
cryptographic hypothesis to show

01:13:51.691 --> 01:13:56.210
that these two clients

01:13:56.211 --> 01:13:58.120
of this library are
indistinguishable except

01:13:58.121 --> 01:14:00.060
for a security parameter,

01:14:00.061 --> 01:14:03.300
probability determined
by a security parameter.

01:14:03.301 --> 01:14:05.100
Having done that, we can

01:14:05.101 --> 01:14:09.000
then approve the
ideal version of TLS.

01:14:09.001 --> 01:14:11.910
Information theoretically
secure with

01:14:11.911 --> 01:14:14.280
no more cryptography
in the picture.

01:14:15.200 --> 01:14:17.855
With that step of
the proof complete,

01:14:17.856 --> 01:14:20.245
you can then show
that an attacker

01:14:20.246 --> 01:14:21.645
cannot distinguish between

01:14:21.646 --> 01:14:23.620
concrete TLS and ideal TLS

01:14:23.621 --> 01:14:26.795
except also for
some probability epsilon.

01:14:26.796 --> 01:14:29.270
So, that's our general
proof methodology

01:14:29.271 --> 01:14:30.715
at the top level.

01:14:30.716 --> 01:14:33.760
In order to apply
this methodology,

01:14:33.761 --> 01:14:35.810
there are various
proof steps that

01:14:35.811 --> 01:14:37.330
become necessary along the way.

01:14:37.331 --> 01:14:39.290
So, here's the structure

01:14:39.291 --> 01:14:41.770
of one simple workflow
through TLS.

01:14:41.771 --> 01:14:45.775
So, TLS is a socket-like API.

01:14:45.776 --> 01:14:47.250
You connect.

01:14:47.251 --> 01:14:49.835
You try to create
a secure socket.

01:14:49.836 --> 01:14:54.220
That triggers the run of
a TLS handshake that's

01:14:54.221 --> 01:14:59.310
going to negotiate keys
between the two endpoints,

01:14:59.450 --> 01:15:02.725
which then requires actually

01:15:02.726 --> 01:15:06.430
sending some plain text
bytes out on the wire

01:15:06.431 --> 01:15:11.960
which will then the TLS
record layer sub protocol

01:15:11.961 --> 01:15:14.890
is going to add some padding
to hide lengths.

01:15:14.891 --> 01:15:16.850
Then finally at the very bottom,

01:15:16.851 --> 01:15:20.290
there's going to be the addition
of a ciphertext and a

01:15:20.291 --> 01:15:25.565
Mac to protect the actual
message that goes out.

01:15:25.566 --> 01:15:27.665
So, at each level,

01:15:27.666 --> 01:15:29.815
we have various
proof obligations.

01:15:29.816 --> 01:15:31.080
So, at the very bottom,

01:15:31.081 --> 01:15:32.870
you need to prove that the
crypto that you actually

01:15:32.871 --> 01:15:35.810
implement is
functionally correct.

01:15:35.870 --> 01:15:39.050
We actually also prove
that it's resistant to

01:15:39.051 --> 01:15:41.640
a class of timing side channels.

01:15:41.641 --> 01:15:44.575
So, as Adam alluded to,

01:15:44.576 --> 01:15:47.545
we have a library called HACL*.

01:15:47.546 --> 01:15:49.395
That's a library implemented

01:15:49.396 --> 01:15:52.440
in the sea-like subset of F*,

01:15:52.441 --> 01:15:54.840
where we have proofs of

01:15:54.841 --> 01:15:59.850
many crypto algorithms
whose performance

01:15:59.851 --> 01:16:02.730
compares quite favorably with the

01:16:02.731 --> 01:16:08.650
best hand-written C. So,

01:16:08.651 --> 01:16:10.140
it's actually
interesting I hadn't

01:16:10.141 --> 01:16:12.230
seen Adam's most recent slide.

01:16:12.231 --> 01:16:16.360
It looks like their

01:16:16.361 --> 01:16:18.370
version of curve is
slightly faster than ours,

01:16:18.371 --> 01:16:26.640
but this 64-bit version of
curve is significantly faster.

01:16:26.641 --> 01:16:28.885
Then it's like 2x faster than

01:16:28.886 --> 01:16:33.240
a hand-written version of
the same curve in OpenSSL,

01:16:33.241 --> 01:16:35.180
which was a 32-bit version

01:16:35.181 --> 01:16:37.970
of the curve that
was the only one

01:16:37.971 --> 01:16:44.245
that OpenSSL had until recently
at the level of C. So,

01:16:44.246 --> 01:16:45.750
using our verification
technology,

01:16:45.751 --> 01:16:47.435
we are able to write

01:16:47.436 --> 01:16:49.820
64-bit versions of the code

01:16:49.821 --> 01:16:52.900
and be confident that
we got it right.

01:16:53.520 --> 01:16:56.020
It's actually this code,

01:16:56.021 --> 01:16:57.610
this code from this library,

01:16:57.611 --> 01:16:59.940
these are crypto library in C,

01:16:59.941 --> 01:17:02.340
is the code that we have
deployed in Firefox.

01:17:02.341 --> 01:17:05.580
So, we have the curve 25519,

01:17:05.581 --> 01:17:09.490
but also some other algorithms,

01:17:09.491 --> 01:17:16.000
ChaCha20 and Poly1305 are
deployed from HACL* in Firefox.

01:17:16.001 --> 01:17:19.395
That said, the code

01:17:19.396 --> 01:17:22.330
generated from HACL is
competitive with C,

01:17:22.331 --> 01:17:24.320
but the best implementations

01:17:24.321 --> 01:17:26.775
in assembly are still
significantly faster.

01:17:26.776 --> 01:17:30.285
So, using Vale, we have verified

01:17:30.286 --> 01:17:35.670
the assembly code in
OpenSSL more or less as is,

01:17:35.671 --> 01:17:39.210
and as you'd expect if we get

01:17:39.211 --> 01:17:42.455
performance that is competitive

01:17:42.456 --> 01:17:46.870
or identical to the OpenSSL code
that we started from.

01:17:47.930 --> 01:17:52.345
At the level of data streams,

01:17:52.346 --> 01:17:58.090
we prove that
a distributed invariant

01:17:58.091 --> 01:18:02.640
that the reader
enter the protocol,

01:18:02.641 --> 01:18:03.830
reads from a prefix of

01:18:03.831 --> 01:18:06.520
the stream that the writer
has sent so far.

01:18:09.400 --> 01:18:12.490
In order to send
messages down the wire,

01:18:12.491 --> 01:18:13.900
we need to prove that parsing

01:18:13.901 --> 01:18:16.275
and formatting code are correct.

01:18:16.276 --> 01:18:18.220
At the high level
domain invariant

01:18:18.221 --> 01:18:19.470
that we're after is that if

01:18:19.471 --> 01:18:22.620
the application sends a message
m and it gets formatted,

01:18:22.621 --> 01:18:25.610
then encrypted and
decrypted and then parsed,

01:18:25.611 --> 01:18:27.370
the receiver receives

01:18:27.371 --> 01:18:29.900
exactly the same message
that was sent.

01:18:30.320 --> 01:18:34.050
The way we do this is rather than

01:18:34.051 --> 01:18:36.790
writing parsers and
formatters by hand,

01:18:36.791 --> 01:18:40.285
which can be very
tedious and error prone,

01:18:40.286 --> 01:18:42.695
we start from
a high-level description

01:18:42.696 --> 01:18:44.460
of the message formats from

01:18:44.461 --> 01:18:50.715
RFC text and synthesize
via metaprogramming,

01:18:50.716 --> 01:18:54.325
a low-level implementation in C

01:18:54.326 --> 01:18:58.945
of parsers and formatters
for those message formats.

01:18:58.946 --> 01:19:03.440
So, I'm running out of
time. I should wrap up.

01:19:03.441 --> 01:19:07.960
So, I'll finish with
some high-level perspective.

01:19:07.961 --> 01:19:11.290
So, we're building
verified components

01:19:11.291 --> 01:19:13.475
motivated primarily by HTTPS,

01:19:13.476 --> 01:19:16.420
but in doing this,
our components that we

01:19:16.421 --> 01:19:19.220
produce are reusable in
other contexts as well.

01:19:19.221 --> 01:19:21.535
So, Quic is one such example

01:19:21.536 --> 01:19:23.200
that I mentioned briefly earlier,

01:19:23.201 --> 01:19:24.765
but some of the work that we're

01:19:24.766 --> 01:19:26.840
currently doing
also involves using

01:19:26.841 --> 01:19:30.160
our crypto libraries to
build things like verified

01:19:30.161 --> 01:19:31.990
authenticated data
structures that are used

01:19:31.991 --> 01:19:35.090
in things like blockchains.

01:19:35.091 --> 01:19:37.630
Recently, there's
some interest in

01:19:37.631 --> 01:19:39.545
using our crypto
libraries to verify

01:19:39.546 --> 01:19:41.540
the correctness and security

01:19:41.541 --> 01:19:44.030
of post-quantum
crypto algorithms.

01:19:44.031 --> 01:19:46.930
Our verified parser and
formatted generators,

01:19:46.931 --> 01:19:48.915
although we're using them in TLS.

01:19:48.916 --> 01:19:51.200
We also see that they have

01:19:51.201 --> 01:19:54.050
applications in other secure
messaging scenarios for

01:19:54.051 --> 01:19:57.050
instance inside a kernel

01:19:57.051 --> 01:20:01.120
or in communications between
VM and kernel and so on.

01:20:04.530 --> 01:20:07.430
Another high-level
perspective about

01:20:07.431 --> 01:20:09.070
Everest is that
rather than trying

01:20:09.071 --> 01:20:13.100
to build a completely secure
stack from the ground up,

01:20:13.101 --> 01:20:15.280
what we're after is building

01:20:15.281 --> 01:20:16.655
verified islands of trust

01:20:16.656 --> 01:20:18.880
inside the existing
software ecosystem,

01:20:18.881 --> 01:20:21.250
and we think there's
value in doing

01:20:21.251 --> 01:20:24.245
that in a way verification is
a lot about the blame game.

01:20:24.246 --> 01:20:27.320
If we deploy our code

01:20:27.321 --> 01:20:30.700
inside an existing
operating system

01:20:30.701 --> 01:20:32.630
Linux or Windows or whatever,

01:20:32.631 --> 01:20:36.330
the entire system is
still vulnerable to bugs,

01:20:36.331 --> 01:20:39.545
but any such bug is going
to be outside of our code.

01:20:39.546 --> 01:20:41.910
If a patch has to be shipped,

01:20:41.911 --> 01:20:42.780
it's not us that's going to be

01:20:42.781 --> 01:20:44.840
shipping a patch,
someone else will.

01:20:44.841 --> 01:20:47.630
Now, finding these islands of

01:20:47.631 --> 01:20:50.030
trust in which
verification can add

01:20:50.031 --> 01:20:53.580
value requires
some careful thinking

01:20:53.581 --> 01:20:55.450
and we think that standards-based

01:20:55.451 --> 01:20:56.570
cryptography and networking is

01:20:56.571 --> 01:20:58.035
a really good match for it,

01:20:58.036 --> 01:20:59.675
partly because there's

01:20:59.676 --> 01:21:03.120
a very widely accepted
common wisdom

01:21:03.121 --> 01:21:04.500
that you're supposed to leave

01:21:04.501 --> 01:21:07.140
crypto to experts and
not try it all your own.

01:21:07.141 --> 01:21:11.675
So, there's an opportunity for

01:21:11.676 --> 01:21:15.740
a well-designed
crypto library and

01:21:15.741 --> 01:21:17.485
protocol library to be used as

01:21:17.486 --> 01:21:18.790
a drop-in replacement for

01:21:18.791 --> 01:21:20.600
the existing code
that's out there.

01:21:20.601 --> 01:21:22.610
Also, I think another

01:21:22.611 --> 01:21:23.710
thing that we're
quite encouraged by

01:21:23.711 --> 01:21:26.580
is that the standards process at

01:21:26.581 --> 01:21:29.620
the IETF and elsewhere is

01:21:29.621 --> 01:21:32.170
increasingly receptive
to this kind of

01:21:32.171 --> 01:21:34.505
formal spec and and validation,

01:21:34.506 --> 01:21:36.590
partly because prior versions

01:21:36.591 --> 01:21:40.175
of these protocols
have really been badly

01:21:40.176 --> 01:21:46.260
beaten up by a cycle of
a taxon and patches.

01:21:46.261 --> 01:21:48.555
So, we think there's
an opportunity there.

01:21:48.556 --> 01:21:51.180
So, that's it.

01:21:51.181 --> 01:21:52.480
I'll leave you with that image.

01:21:52.481 --> 01:21:55.740
It's our goal of
trying to build and

01:21:55.741 --> 01:22:00.850
deploy verified
components in HTTPS.

01:22:14.010 --> 01:22:16.100
&gt;&gt; Hi, Rebecca Wright,

01:22:16.101 --> 01:22:18.850
Rutgers University.
Thanks for the talk.

01:22:18.851 --> 01:22:23.180
So, you noted at some point
that of course all of

01:22:23.181 --> 01:22:25.780
these proofs are dependent
on the model and

01:22:25.781 --> 01:22:27.030
that there is always the

01:22:27.031 --> 01:22:28.870
potential for attacks
outside of the model.

01:22:28.871 --> 01:22:31.400
In the practical usage here,

01:22:31.401 --> 01:22:33.630
do you have sort of a sense

01:22:33.631 --> 01:22:36.260
whether it's sort of inherently
a cat and mouse game that

01:22:36.261 --> 01:22:37.960
will continue
indefinitely or whether

01:22:37.961 --> 01:22:39.920
they're in a practical
sense some kind of

01:22:39.921 --> 01:22:41.770
convergence to capturing most of

01:22:41.771 --> 01:22:44.620
the meaningful potential attacks?

01:22:44.700 --> 01:22:47.290
&gt;&gt; I think at a certain level,

01:22:47.291 --> 01:22:53.760
there's a high level spec

01:22:53.761 --> 01:22:55.415
about what the protocol
is trying to achieve.

01:22:55.416 --> 01:22:59.040
I think we're reaching
a convergence,

01:22:59.041 --> 01:23:01.940
but there's always
the possibility of

01:23:01.941 --> 01:23:04.135
vulnerabilities at
the lowest level

01:23:04.136 --> 01:23:05.990
that our models
don't account for.

01:23:05.991 --> 01:23:10.450
So, for instance, we have
proofs of resistance

01:23:10.451 --> 01:23:13.190
against a certain class
of side channel attacks,

01:23:13.191 --> 01:23:17.060
where we didn't anticipate
spectre and meltdown either.

01:23:17.061 --> 01:23:19.195
So, although we think our

01:23:19.196 --> 01:23:22.195
code for the particular kind
of code that you write,

01:23:22.196 --> 01:23:23.380
it's not vulnerable to

01:23:23.381 --> 01:23:25.425
these kinds of spectre
and meltdown attacks,

01:23:25.426 --> 01:23:28.310
our proofs certainly
don't account for this.

01:23:28.530 --> 01:23:31.980
So, those kinds of

01:23:31.981 --> 01:23:35.570
scenarios may still
occur in the future.

01:23:42.730 --> 01:23:45.870
&gt;&gt; Another questions.

01:23:49.020 --> 01:23:51.460
Okay. Well, let's thank

01:23:51.461 --> 01:23:54.410
the speaker again
and head for lunch.

